module.exports = [
  { q: ["How much is a 15% tip on a $48 bill?","15 percent tip on 48 dollars","whats the tip on $48 if i leave 15%"], a: "A 15% tip on $48 is $7.20.\n\nWorking: 48 × 0.15 = 7.20\n\nSo your total would be $48 + $7.20 = $55.20. Quick mental trick: 10% of $48 is $4.80, half of that (5%) is $2.40, and $4.80 + $2.40 = $7.20.", lang: "en", topic: "math" },
  { q: ["A jacket costs $85 and is 20% off. What's the sale price?","20% off 85 dollars how much do i pay","price after 20 percent discount on $85"], a: "The sale price is $68 (before tax).\n\nWorking:\n- Discount: 85 × 0.20 = $17\n- Sale price: 85 − 17 = $68\n\nShortcut: paying 80% of the price gives the same answer, since 85 × 0.80 = 68.", lang: "en", topic: "math" },
  { q: ["How do I calculate a percentage increase from 40 to 50?","percent increase 40 to 50","my rent went from 40 to 50 hundred... how do you work out the % change"], a: "Going from 40 to 50 is a 25% increase.\n\nFormula: (new − old) ÷ old × 100\n\nWorking: (50 − 40) ÷ 40 × 100 = 10 ÷ 40 × 100 = 25%\n\nNote that the reverse isn't symmetric: going from 50 down to 40 is a 20% decrease, because you divide by 50 instead of 40.", lang: "en", topic: "math" },
  { q: ["What percent of 72 is 18?","18 is what percentage of 72","18 out of 72 as a percent"], a: "18 is 25% of 72.\n\nWorking: 18 ÷ 72 = 0.25, and 0.25 × 100 = 25%.\n\nCheck: 25% of 72 is 72 × 0.25 = 18. ✓\n\nGeneral formula: part ÷ whole × 100.", lang: "en", topic: "math" },
  { q: ["How do I add 1/3 and 1/4?","1/3 + 1/4","adding fractions with different denominators help"], a: "1/3 + 1/4 = 7/12.\n\nSteps:\n1. Find a common denominator: the lowest common multiple of 3 and 4 is 12.\n2. Convert: 1/3 = 4/12 and 1/4 = 3/12.\n3. Add the numerators: 4/12 + 3/12 = 7/12.\n\n7/12 can't be simplified further, since 7 and 12 share no common factor.", lang: "en", topic: "math" },
  { q: ["How do you multiply fractions like 2/3 times 3/4?","2/3 x 3/4","multiply 2 thirds by 3 quarters"], a: "2/3 × 3/4 = 1/2.\n\nSteps:\n1. Multiply the tops: 2 × 3 = 6\n2. Multiply the bottoms: 3 × 4 = 12\n3. Simplify: 6/12 = 1/2\n\nUnlike adding, you don't need a common denominator to multiply fractions.", lang: "en", topic: "math" },
  { q: ["How do I divide 3/4 by 1/2?","3/4 divided by 1/2","dividing fractions keep change flip?"], a: "3/4 ÷ 1/2 = 3/2, which is 1½ or 1.5.\n\nMethod (\"keep, change, flip\"):\n1. Keep the first fraction: 3/4\n2. Change ÷ to ×\n3. Flip the second fraction: 1/2 becomes 2/1\n\n3/4 × 2/1 = 6/4 = 3/2\n\nCheck: half of 1.5 is 0.75, which is 3/4. ✓", lang: "en", topic: "math" },
  { q: ["Solve 3x + 7 = 22","how do i solve 3x+7=22","find x: 3x plus 7 equals 22"], a: "x = 5.\n\nSteps:\n1. Subtract 7 from both sides: 3x = 15\n2. Divide both sides by 3: x = 5\n\nCheck: 3(5) + 7 = 15 + 7 = 22. ✓\n\nThe idea is to undo operations in reverse order, doing the same thing to both sides each time.", lang: "en", topic: "math" },
  { q: ["Solve 2(x − 4) = 10","2(x-4)=10 solve for x","how to solve an equation with brackets 2(x-4)=10"], a: "x = 9.\n\nSteps:\n1. Divide both sides by 2: x − 4 = 5\n2. Add 4 to both sides: x = 9\n\nAlternative: expand first, 2x − 8 = 10, so 2x = 18 and x = 9.\n\nCheck: 2(9 − 4) = 2(5) = 10. ✓", lang: "en", topic: "math" },
  { q: ["Solve 5x − 3 = 2x + 9","x on both sides 5x-3=2x+9","how do I solve when there's x on both sides"], a: "x = 4.\n\nSteps:\n1. Subtract 2x from both sides: 3x − 3 = 9\n2. Add 3 to both sides: 3x = 12\n3. Divide by 3: x = 4\n\nCheck: left side 5(4) − 3 = 17; right side 2(4) + 9 = 17. ✓\n\nTip: gather all the x terms on one side and the plain numbers on the other.", lang: "en", topic: "math" },
  { q: ["Solve x² − 5x + 6 = 0","factor x^2 - 5x + 6","how do i solve x squared minus 5x plus 6 equals 0"], a: "x = 2 or x = 3.\n\nSteps (factoring):\n1. Find two numbers that multiply to 6 and add to −5: those are −2 and −3.\n2. Factor: (x − 2)(x − 3) = 0\n3. Set each factor to zero: x − 2 = 0 or x − 3 = 0\n\nCheck: 2² − 5(2) + 6 = 4 − 10 + 6 = 0 ✓ and 3² − 5(3) + 6 = 9 − 15 + 6 = 0 ✓", lang: "en", topic: "math" },
  { q: ["Use the quadratic formula to solve 2x² + 3x − 2 = 0","quadratic formula example please","how does the quadratic formula work"], a: "x = 1/2 or x = −2.\n\nFormula: x = [−b ± √(b² − 4ac)] ÷ 2a\n\nHere a = 2, b = 3, c = −2.\n1. Discriminant: b² − 4ac = 9 − 4(2)(−2) = 9 + 16 = 25\n2. √25 = 5\n3. x = (−3 + 5) ÷ 4 = 2/4 = 1/2\n4. x = (−3 − 5) ÷ 4 = −8/4 = −2\n\nCheck: 2(1/4) + 3(1/2) − 2 = 0.5 + 1.5 − 2 = 0 ✓", lang: "en", topic: "math" },
  { q: ["Solve x² = 49","x squared equals 49","what is x if x^2=49"], a: "x = 7 or x = −7.\n\nTake the square root of both sides, remembering both the positive and negative roots: x = ±√49 = ±7.\n\nCheck: 7 × 7 = 49 and (−7) × (−7) = 49. ✓\n\nA common mistake is giving only 7; a squared value always has two real roots when it's positive.", lang: "en", topic: "math" },
  { q: ["What is the discriminant in a quadratic equation?","what does b^2-4ac tell you","discriminant meaning"], a: "The discriminant is b² − 4ac, the part under the square root in the quadratic formula. It tells you how many real solutions ax² + bx + c = 0 has:\n- Positive: two different real solutions\n- Zero: one repeated solution\n- Negative: no real solutions (two complex ones)\n\nExample: x² + 4x + 4 = 0 has b² − 4ac = 16 − 16 = 0, so it has one solution, x = −2, since it factors as (x + 2)².", lang: "en", topic: "math" },
  { q: ["Solve the system x + y = 10 and x − y = 4","system of equations x+y=10 x-y=4","two equations two unknowns how"], a: "x = 7 and y = 3.\n\nElimination method:\n1. Add the two equations: (x + y) + (x − y) = 10 + 4, so 2x = 14\n2. x = 7\n3. Substitute into x + y = 10: 7 + y = 10, so y = 3\n\nCheck: 7 + 3 = 10 ✓ and 7 − 3 = 4 ✓", lang: "en", topic: "math" },
  { q: ["Solve 2x + y = 11 and x + 3y = 13","substitution method example 2x+y=11, x+3y=13","how to do substitution for systems"], a: "x = 4 and y = 3.\n\nSubstitution method:\n1. From the first equation: y = 11 − 2x\n2. Substitute into the second: x + 3(11 − 2x) = 13\n3. Expand: x + 33 − 6x = 13, so −5x = −20\n4. x = 4, then y = 11 − 2(4) = 3\n\nCheck: 2(4) + 3 = 11 ✓ and 4 + 3(3) = 13 ✓", lang: "en", topic: "math" },
  { q: ["What are the main exponent rules?","exponent laws","how do powers work when multiplying like 2^3 x 2^4"], a: "Key exponent rules (same base):\n- Multiply: add exponents. 2³ × 2⁴ = 2⁷ = 128\n- Divide: subtract exponents. 5⁶ ÷ 5⁴ = 5² = 25\n- Power of a power: multiply exponents. (3²)³ = 3⁶ = 729\n- Zero exponent: any non-zero number to the power 0 is 1\n- Negative exponent: means a reciprocal. 2⁻³ = 1/2³ = 1/8\n\nThese only work when the bases are the same.", lang: "en", topic: "math" },
  { q: ["What is a logarithm?","explain logs simply","what does log base 2 of 32 mean"], a: "A logarithm answers: \"what power do I raise the base to, to get this number?\"\n\n- log₂(32) = 5, because 2⁵ = 32\n- log₁₀(1000) = 3, because 10³ = 1000\n- log₁₀(1) = 0, because 10⁰ = 1\n\nSo logs are the reverse of exponents. Useful rules: log(ab) = log a + log b, and log(aⁿ) = n·log a.", lang: "en", topic: "math" },
  { q: ["What is ln and what is e?","natural log explained","what's the number e in math"], a: "e is a special constant, about 2.71828, that shows up naturally in growth and decay, such as continuously compounded interest and population growth.\n\nln is the natural logarithm, meaning log base e. It answers: \"e to what power gives this number?\"\n- ln(e) = 1\n- ln(1) = 0\n- ln(e³) = 3\n\nOn most calculators, ln is its own button, separate from log (which is base 10).", lang: "en", topic: "math" },
  { q: ["What is the area of a circle with a radius of 5 cm?","area of circle radius 5","how do i find the area of a circle"], a: "The area is 25π cm², about 78.54 cm².\n\nFormula: A = πr²\n\nWorking: π × 5² = π × 25 ≈ 3.14159 × 25 ≈ 78.54 cm²\n\nRelated: the circumference is C = 2πr = 10π ≈ 31.42 cm. If you're given the diameter instead, halve it first to get the radius.", lang: "en", topic: "math" },
  { q: ["Area of a triangle with base 8 and height 5?","how to find triangle area","triangle base 8 cm height 5 cm area"], a: "The area is 20 square units (for example, 20 cm²).\n\nFormula: A = ½ × base × height\n\nWorking: ½ × 8 × 5 = ½ × 40 = 20\n\nThe height must be perpendicular to the base. A triangle is exactly half of a rectangle with the same base and height, which is where the ½ comes from.", lang: "en", topic: "math" },
  { q: ["What is the Pythagorean theorem?","pythagoras legs 6 and 8 find hypotenuse","how to find the long side of a right triangle"], a: "In a right triangle, a² + b² = c², where c is the hypotenuse (the side opposite the right angle).\n\nExample with legs 6 and 8:\n1. 6² + 8² = 36 + 64 = 100\n2. c = √100 = 10\n\nCommon whole-number sets: 3-4-5, 5-12-13, 8-15-17. It only works for triangles with a 90° angle.", lang: "en", topic: "math" },
  { q: ["A 13 m ladder leans against a wall with its base 5 m from the wall. How high does it reach?","ladder problem pythagoras 13 and 5","ladder 13 metres base 5 metres away how high up the wall"], a: "The ladder reaches 12 m up the wall.\n\nThe ladder is the hypotenuse (13 m), and the ground distance is one leg (5 m).\n1. h² + 5² = 13²\n2. h² + 25 = 169\n3. h² = 144\n4. h = 12 m\n\nCheck: 5² + 12² = 25 + 144 = 169 = 13². ✓", lang: "en", topic: "math" },
  { q: ["Area and perimeter of a 12 m by 7 m room?","how much flooring for a 12x7 room","rectangle 12 by 7 area perimeter"], a: "Area = 84 m²; perimeter = 38 m.\n\nWorking:\n- Area = length × width = 12 × 7 = 84 m² (useful for flooring or paint estimates)\n- Perimeter = 2 × (length + width) = 2 × (12 + 7) = 2 × 19 = 38 m (useful for baseboards)\n\nTip: buy roughly 10% extra flooring to allow for cuts and waste.", lang: "en", topic: "math" },
  { q: ["How do I find the volume of a box and a cylinder?","volume of cylinder radius 3 height 10","volume formulas"], a: "Box (rectangular prism): V = length × width × height. Example: 4 × 3 × 2 = 24 cubic units.\n\nCylinder: V = πr²h. Example with r = 3 and h = 10:\nπ × 3² × 10 = 90π ≈ 282.74 cubic units.\n\nHandy conversion: 1,000 cm³ = 1 litre, so a container with a volume of 2,000 cm³ holds 2 L.", lang: "en", topic: "math" },
  { q: ["Find the mean, median and mode of 4, 7, 7, 9, 13","mean median mode example","whats the difference between mean median and mode"], a: "Mean = 8, median = 7, mode = 7.\n\n- Mean (average): add them and divide by the count. 4 + 7 + 7 + 9 + 13 = 40; 40 ÷ 5 = 8\n- Median: the middle value when sorted. The list is already in order, so it's 7\n- Mode: the most frequent value. 7 appears twice\n\nThe median is less affected by extreme values, which is why it's often used for things like home prices.", lang: "en", topic: "math" },
  { q: ["How do you find the median with an even number of values?","median of 3 5 8 10","median when there are 4 numbers"], a: "Take the two middle values and average them.\n\nExample: 3, 5, 8, 10\n1. Make sure the list is sorted (it is).\n2. The two middle values are 5 and 8.\n3. Median = (5 + 8) ÷ 2 = 13 ÷ 2 = 6.5\n\nThe median doesn't have to be one of the numbers in the list.", lang: "en", topic: "math" },
  { q: ["What's the probability of rolling a 6 on a die?","probability of getting two heads flipping two coins","basic probability explained"], a: "Rolling a 6 on a fair six-sided die: 1/6, about 16.7%.\n\nProbability = favourable outcomes ÷ total possible outcomes.\n\nTwo heads with two fair coins: the outcomes are HH, HT, TH, TT, so P(HH) = 1/4 = 25%. Equivalently, for independent events you multiply: ½ × ½ = ¼.\n\nProbabilities always fall between 0 (impossible) and 1 (certain).", lang: "en", topic: "math" },
  { q: ["What's the probability of drawing a heart from a deck of cards?","chance of pulling a heart from 52 cards","probability ace from a deck"], a: "The probability of drawing a heart is 13/52 = 1/4, or 25%.\n\nA standard deck has 52 cards in 4 suits of 13 each.\n\nOther examples:\n- An ace: 4/52 = 1/13, about 7.7%\n- A red card: 26/52 = 1/2, or 50%\n- The ace of spades: 1/52, about 1.9%", lang: "en", topic: "math" },
  { q: ["How many miles is 100 km?","convert km to miles","100 kilometres in miles"], a: "100 km is about 62.1 miles.\n\nConversion: 1 mile = 1.609344 km, so km ÷ 1.609344 = miles (or multiply by about 0.6214).\n\nWorking: 100 × 0.6214 ≈ 62.1 miles\n\nGoing the other way: 60 mph ≈ 60 × 1.609 ≈ 96.6 km/h, which is why 100 km/h on a highway feels close to 60 mph.", lang: "en", topic: "math" },
  { q: ["How do I convert Celsius to Fahrenheit?","20 C in F","celsius to fahrenheit formula"], a: "Use F = C × 9/5 + 32.\n\nExample: 20°C → 20 × 9/5 + 32 = 36 + 32 = 68°F\n\nHandy reference points:\n- 0°C = 32°F (water freezes)\n- 37°C = 98.6°F (normal body temperature)\n- 100°C = 212°F (water boils at sea level)\n- −40°C = −40°F (the two scales meet)\n\nQuick estimate: double the Celsius and add 30.", lang: "en", topic: "math" },
  { q: ["My recipe says 350°F. What is that in Celsius?","fahrenheit to celsius formula","350 F to C oven"], a: "350°F is about 177°C, so most Canadian ovens would use 175 or 180°C.\n\nFormula: C = (F − 32) × 5/9\n\nWorking: (350 − 32) × 5/9 = 318 × 5/9 = 1590/9 ≈ 176.7°C\n\nOther oven temperatures: 325°F ≈ 163°C, 375°F ≈ 191°C, 400°F ≈ 204°C, 425°F ≈ 218°C.", lang: "en", topic: "math" },
  { q: ["How many kilograms is 150 pounds?","lbs to kg","convert my weight 150 lb to kg"], a: "150 lb is about 68 kg.\n\nConversion: 1 kg ≈ 2.2046 lb, so divide pounds by 2.2046.\n\nWorking: 150 ÷ 2.2046 ≈ 68.04 kg\n\nGoing the other way, multiply kilograms by 2.2046: 70 kg ≈ 154.3 lb. A rough mental shortcut is to halve the pounds and subtract 10% of that: 150 → 75 → 75 − 7.5 = 67.5 kg.", lang: "en", topic: "math" },
  { q: ["What is 5 feet 10 inches in centimetres?","convert height feet inches to cm","5'10 in cm"], a: "5 ft 10 in is 177.8 cm.\n\nWorking:\n1. Convert to inches: 5 × 12 + 10 = 70 inches\n2. Multiply by 2.54 (exact): 70 × 2.54 = 177.8 cm\n\nGoing the other way: divide centimetres by 2.54 to get inches, then divide by 12 for feet. For example, 160 cm ÷ 2.54 ≈ 63 inches, which is about 5 ft 3 in.", lang: "en", topic: "math" },
  { q: ["How many litres are in a gallon?","gallon to litres conversion","us gallon vs imperial gallon"], a: "It depends on which gallon:\n- US gallon ≈ 3.785 L\n- Imperial gallon (older Canadian/UK measure) ≈ 4.546 L\n\nExamples:\n- 10 US gallons ≈ 37.85 L\n- 10 imperial gallons ≈ 45.46 L\n\nCanada uses litres for gas and most products, but older recipes or American sources may still use gallons, so check which one is meant.", lang: "en", topic: "math" },
  { q: ["How does simple interest work?","simple interest formula with example","interest on 2000 at 5% for 3 years simple"], a: "Simple interest is calculated only on the original amount (the principal).\n\nFormula: I = P × r × t\n- P = principal\n- r = annual rate as a decimal\n- t = time in years\n\nExample: $2,000 at 5% for 3 years\nI = 2,000 × 0.05 × 3 = $300\nTotal = $2,000 + $300 = $2,300\n\nThis is a math example only; for decisions about your own savings or loans, talk to a licensed financial advisor.", lang: "en", topic: "math" },
  { q: ["Explain compound interest with an example","compound interest formula","how much is 1000 at 5% compounded yearly for 3 years"], a: "Compound interest earns interest on the interest too.\n\nFormula: A = P(1 + r/n)^(nt)\n- P = principal, r = annual rate (decimal), n = compounding periods per year, t = years\n\nExample: $1,000 at 5% compounded yearly for 3 years\nA = 1,000 × 1.05³ = 1,000 × 1.157625 = $1,157.63\n\nYear by year: $1,050.00 → $1,102.50 → $1,157.63. Simple interest would give only $1,150. This is a math illustration, not financial advice.", lang: "en", topic: "math" },
  { q: ["How does monthly compounding change things?","1000 at 6% compounded monthly for a year","compounded monthly vs yearly"], a: "More frequent compounding grows money slightly faster.\n\nExample: $1,000 at 6% for 1 year\n- Yearly: 1,000 × 1.06 = $1,060.00\n- Monthly: rate per month = 0.06 ÷ 12 = 0.005, so A = 1,000 × 1.005¹² ≈ 1,000 × 1.061678 ≈ $1,061.68\n\nThe difference grows over longer periods and larger amounts. For real savings or borrowing decisions, a licensed financial advisor can help.", lang: "en", topic: "math" },
  { q: ["What is the rule of 72?","how long to double money at 6%","rule of 72 explained"], a: "The rule of 72 is a quick estimate of how long it takes something growing at a fixed annual rate to double: years ≈ 72 ÷ rate (as a percentage).\n\nExamples:\n- At 6%: 72 ÷ 6 = about 12 years\n- At 8%: 72 ÷ 8 = about 9 years\n\nCheck at 6%: 1.06¹² ≈ 2.01, so it works well. It's an approximation for mental math, not a prediction of real returns.", lang: "en", topic: "math" },
  { q: ["What is a derivative in calculus?","derivative of 3x^2 + 2x - 5","power rule derivatives explained"], a: "A derivative measures how fast something changes, like the slope of a curve at a point.\n\nPower rule: d/dx (xⁿ) = n·xⁿ⁻¹\n\nExample: d/dx (3x² + 2x − 5)\n- 3x² → 3 × 2x = 6x\n- 2x → 2\n- −5 (a constant) → 0\nResult: 6x + 2\n\nAt x = 1, the slope is 6(1) + 2 = 8. In physics, the derivative of position over time is velocity.", lang: "en", topic: "math" },
  { q: ["What is an integral?","integrate x^2 from 0 to 3","basic integration explained"], a: "An integral is the reverse of a derivative and can measure the area under a curve.\n\nPower rule: ∫xⁿ dx = xⁿ⁺¹ ÷ (n + 1) + C, for n ≠ −1\n\nExamples:\n- ∫2x dx = x² + C (C is the constant of integration)\n- Definite integral from 0 to 3 of x² dx: [x³ ÷ 3] from 0 to 3 = 27/3 − 0 = 9\n\nSo the area under y = x² between x = 0 and x = 3 is 9 square units.", lang: "en", topic: "math" },
  { q: ["If I drive 240 km in 3 hours, what's my average speed?","speed distance time formula","how long to drive 400 km at 100 km/h"], a: "Your average speed is 80 km/h.\n\nFormula: speed = distance ÷ time\nWorking: 240 ÷ 3 = 80 km/h\n\nRearranged:\n- time = distance ÷ speed: 400 km at 100 km/h takes 400 ÷ 100 = 4 hours\n- distance = speed × time: 90 km/h for 2.5 hours covers 225 km\n\nReal trips take longer because of stops, traffic and weather.", lang: "en", topic: "math" },
  { q: ["One person paints a room in 6 hours, another in 3 hours. How long together?","work rate problem two painters","how do work together problems work"], a: "Together they take 2 hours.\n\nWork-rate method:\n1. Person A does 1/6 of the room per hour; person B does 1/3.\n2. Combined rate: 1/6 + 1/3 = 1/6 + 2/6 = 3/6 = 1/2 room per hour.\n3. Time = 1 ÷ (1/2) = 2 hours.\n\nCheck: in 2 hours A does 2/6 and B does 2/3 = 4/6; 2/6 + 4/6 = 1 whole room. ✓", lang: "en", topic: "math" },
  { q: ["Which is the better deal: 500 g for $4.00 or 750 g for $5.70?","how to compare unit prices","unit price grocery math"], a: "The 750 g package is the better deal.\n\nCompare the price per 100 g:\n- 500 g for $4.00: 4.00 ÷ 5 = $0.80 per 100 g\n- 750 g for $5.70: 5.70 ÷ 7.5 = $0.76 per 100 g\n\nMany Canadian grocery shelf labels already show a unit price, which makes this quicker. A bigger package only saves money if you'll use it before it spoils.", lang: "en", topic: "math" },
  { q: ["What's the order of operations?","BEDMAS explained","what is 6 + 4 × 3 − (2 + 1)²"], a: "In Canada it's usually taught as BEDMAS: Brackets, Exponents, Division and Multiplication (left to right), Addition and Subtraction (left to right).\n\nExample: 6 + 4 × 3 − (2 + 1)²\n1. Brackets: (2 + 1) = 3\n2. Exponents: 3² = 9\n3. Multiplication: 4 × 3 = 12\n4. Left to right: 6 + 12 − 9 = 9\n\nAnswer: 9. Doing it strictly left to right would give a wrong answer.", lang: "en", topic: "math" },
  { q: ["What is Newton's first law?","newtons 1st law simple explanation","what's the law of inertia"], a: "Newton's first law (the law of inertia) says an object stays at rest, or keeps moving at the same speed in a straight line, unless a force acts on it.\n\nEveryday example: when a bus brakes suddenly, your body keeps moving forward. That's why seatbelts matter. On Earth, moving things usually slow down because of forces like friction and air resistance, not because motion naturally stops.", lang: "en", topic: "science" },
  { q: ["Explain Newton's second law","what does F = ma mean","force equals mass times acceleration example"], a: "Newton's second law says force = mass × acceleration (F = ma). The more force you apply, the more an object accelerates; the heavier it is, the less it accelerates for the same force.\n\nExample: pushing a 10 kg cart to accelerate it at 2 m/s² needs 10 × 2 = 20 newtons of force.\n\nThis is why a loaded grocery cart is harder to get moving than an empty one.", lang: "en", topic: "science" },
  { q: ["What is Newton's third law?","for every action there is an equal and opposite reaction meaning","newton third law examples"], a: "Newton's third law says that when one object pushes on another, the second pushes back with an equal force in the opposite direction.\n\nExamples:\n- A rocket pushes exhaust gas down; the gas pushes the rocket up.\n- When you skate, you push back on the ice and it pushes you forward.\n- A rifle recoils when it fires.\n\nThe two forces act on different objects, which is why they don't cancel out.", lang: "en", topic: "science" },
  { q: ["Why do things fall?","what is gravity","how strong is gravity on earth"], a: "Things fall because of gravity, the attraction between objects that have mass. Earth is so massive that it pulls everything near it toward its centre.\n\nNear Earth's surface, falling objects speed up by about 9.8 metres per second every second (9.8 m/s²), ignoring air resistance. Without air, a feather and a hammer fall at the same rate. Astronauts showed this on the Moon. Gravity also keeps the Moon orbiting Earth and Earth orbiting the Sun.", lang: "en", topic: "science" },
  { q: ["Why do astronauts float in space if there's gravity?","is there no gravity on the space station","why are astronauts weightless"], a: "There is still plenty of gravity at the space station's altitude, roughly 90% of what we feel on the ground. Astronauts float because they're in free fall.\n\nThe station and everyone in it are constantly falling toward Earth, but they're moving sideways so fast that they keep missing it. That's what an orbit is. Since everything falls together, nothing presses against the floor, so they feel weightless.", lang: "en", topic: "science" },
  { q: ["What's the difference between kinetic and potential energy?","kinetic vs potential energy","explain energy types simply"], a: "Kinetic energy is the energy of motion; potential energy is stored energy.\n\n- Kinetic: a moving car, a thrown ball. It increases with mass and especially speed (KE = ½mv²), so doubling your speed quadruples it.\n- Potential: a book on a high shelf (gravitational), a stretched elastic (elastic), or food and fuel (chemical).\n\nOn a toboggan hill, potential energy at the top turns into kinetic energy as you slide down.", lang: "en", topic: "science" },
  { q: ["What is the law of conservation of energy?","can energy be created or destroyed","energy conservation physics meaning"], a: "Energy can't be created or destroyed; it only changes form. The total amount in a closed system stays the same.\n\nExamples:\n- A hydro dam turns the energy of falling water into electricity.\n- A light bulb turns electrical energy into light and heat.\n- Your body turns chemical energy from food into movement and warmth.\n\nWhen energy seems \"lost,\" it has usually become heat, which is harder to use.", lang: "en", topic: "science" },
  { q: ["What are voltage, current and resistance?","ohm's law explained","if a 12 volt battery has 4 ohms resistance how much current"], a: "Think of electricity like water in a pipe:\n- Voltage (volts, V) is the push, like water pressure.\n- Current (amps, A) is how much charge flows, like the flow rate.\n- Resistance (ohms, Ω) is how hard it is to flow, like a narrow pipe.\n\nOhm's law: V = I × R. With 12 V and 4 Ω, the current is I = 12 ÷ 4 = 3 A.\n\nPower (watts) = V × I, so that circuit uses 36 W.", lang: "en", topic: "science" },
  { q: ["What's the difference between AC and DC?","ac vs dc electricity","why do houses use alternating current"], a: "DC (direct current) flows in one direction. Batteries, phones and laptops run on DC.\n\nAC (alternating current) switches direction many times per second. In Canada, household outlets supply AC at 60 cycles per second (60 Hz).\n\nGrids use AC mainly because transformers can easily raise its voltage for efficient long-distance transmission and lower it again for homes. Your phone charger converts the outlet's AC to DC.", lang: "en", topic: "science" },
  { q: ["Why is the sky blue?","why is the sky blue during the day but red at sunset","sky colour science"], a: "Sunlight contains all colours. When it passes through the air, tiny gas molecules scatter shorter wavelengths (blue) much more than longer ones (red). That scattered blue light reaches your eyes from every direction, so the sky looks blue.\n\nAt sunrise and sunset, sunlight travels through much more atmosphere. Most of the blue gets scattered away before reaching you, leaving the oranges and reds. This effect is called Rayleigh scattering.", lang: "en", topic: "science" },
  { q: ["How do rainbows form?","why do we see rainbows","what causes a rainbow"], a: "A rainbow forms when sunlight enters raindrops, bends (refracts), reflects off the back of the drop, and bends again as it leaves. Each colour bends by a slightly different amount, so white light spreads into red, orange, yellow, green, blue, indigo and violet.\n\nTo see one, the Sun must be behind you and rain in front of you. That's why rainbows often appear in late afternoon after a shower.", lang: "en", topic: "science" },
  { q: ["How fast does light travel?","speed of light","how long does sunlight take to reach earth"], a: "Light travels at about 300,000 km per second in a vacuum (more precisely, 299,792 km/s). Nothing with mass can go faster.\n\nAt that speed:\n- Light could circle Earth about 7.5 times in one second.\n- Sunlight takes about 8 minutes and 20 seconds to reach Earth.\n- Moonlight takes about 1.3 seconds.\n\nSo when you look at the Sun, you're seeing it as it was about 8 minutes ago.", lang: "en", topic: "science" },
  { q: ["Why do you see lightning before you hear thunder?","how to tell how far away a storm is","count seconds after lightning distance"], a: "Light travels almost instantly, but sound moves much more slowly, about 343 metres per second in air (roughly 1 km every 3 seconds).\n\nTo estimate the distance: count the seconds between the flash and the thunder, then divide by 3 to get kilometres. Nine seconds means the storm is about 3 km away.\n\nIf you can hear thunder, you're close enough to be struck. Go indoors and wait about 30 minutes after the last thunder.", lang: "en", topic: "science" },
  { q: ["What is an atom made of?","explain atoms protons neutrons electrons","what's inside an atom"], a: "An atom has a tiny, dense nucleus surrounded by electrons.\n\n- Protons: positive charge, in the nucleus. The number of protons defines the element (carbon always has 6).\n- Neutrons: no charge, in the nucleus. They add mass.\n- Electrons: negative charge, moving around the nucleus. They take part in chemical reactions.\n\nAtoms are mostly empty space. If the nucleus were a marble, the atom would be about the size of a stadium.", lang: "en", topic: "science" },
  { q: ["How does the periodic table work?","periodic table explained simply","what do rows and columns on the periodic table mean"], a: "The periodic table lists all known elements in order of atomic number (the number of protons).\n\n- Rows (periods): each new row adds another electron shell.\n- Columns (groups): elements in a column behave similarly. For example, group 1 metals like sodium react strongly with water, and group 18 noble gases like neon and argon barely react at all.\n\nMetals are on the left and centre, non-metals on the upper right. Dmitri Mendeleev published an early version in 1869.", lang: "en", topic: "science" },
  { q: ["What is pH?","what does acidic or basic mean","ph scale explained"], a: "pH measures how acidic or basic (alkaline) a water-based solution is, on a scale usually from 0 to 14.\n\n- Below 7: acidic (lemon juice, vinegar)\n- 7: neutral (pure water)\n- Above 7: basic (baking soda solution, soap, bleach)\n\nEach step is a factor of 10, so pH 3 is ten times more acidic than pH 4. Never mix bleach with acids or ammonia products; it can release dangerous gases.", lang: "en", topic: "science" },
  { q: ["What happens when you mix baking soda and vinegar?","baking soda vinegar reaction","acid base reaction example"], a: "It's an acid-base reaction. Vinegar (acetic acid) reacts with baking soda (sodium bicarbonate) to make carbon dioxide gas, water and sodium acetate. The fizzing bubbles are the carbon dioxide.\n\nThe same idea makes baked goods rise: baking soda reacts with acidic ingredients like buttermilk, and the gas bubbles puff up the batter. It's a safe, classic science-fair demonstration.", lang: "en", topic: "science" },
  { q: ["What's the difference between a chemical change and a physical change?","chemical vs physical change examples","is melting ice a chemical change"], a: "A physical change alters form or state but not what the substance is. A chemical change creates new substances.\n\nPhysical: melting ice, boiling water, cutting paper, dissolving sugar. Melted ice is still water.\n\nChemical: burning wood, rusting iron, baking a cake, digesting food. Signs include gas being produced, a colour change, light or heat, or a new smell. Chemical changes are usually hard to reverse.", lang: "en", topic: "science" },
  { q: ["Why does ice float?","why does ice float on water","why do lakes freeze from the top"], a: "Ice floats because it's less dense than liquid water. When water freezes, its molecules lock into an open crystal pattern held together by hydrogen bonds, which takes up about 9% more space.\n\nThis is why Canadian lakes freeze from the top down. The ice layer insulates the water below, letting fish survive winter. It's also why water pipes can burst when they freeze.", lang: "en", topic: "science" },
  { q: ["Why do they put salt on roads in winter?","how does road salt melt ice","why does salt melt ice"], a: "Salt lowers the freezing point of water. When salt dissolves into the thin layer of liquid on ice, the resulting brine needs a colder temperature to freeze, so the ice melts.\n\nIt works less well in extreme cold. Ordinary rock salt becomes much less effective below about −10°C, which is why crews may use sand for traction or other de-icers. Salt also speeds up rust, so washing your car in winter helps.", lang: "en", topic: "science" },
  { q: ["Why does water boil at a lower temperature in the mountains?","boiling point altitude","does water boil faster at high altitude"], a: "Water boils when its vapour pressure matches the air pressure around it. At high altitude there's less air pressure, so water boils at a lower temperature.\n\nAt sea level it boils at 100°C. In a mountain town like Banff, at about 1,400 m, it boils at roughly 95°C. Food cooks more slowly because the water isn't as hot, which is why some recipes have high-altitude adjustments.", lang: "en", topic: "science" },
  { q: ["How does photosynthesis work?","what is photosynthesis","how do plants make food"], a: "Photosynthesis is how plants, algae and some bacteria make food from light.\n\nIn simple terms: carbon dioxide + water + light energy → glucose (sugar) + oxygen\n\nIt happens mainly in leaves, inside chloroplasts that contain chlorophyll, the green pigment that absorbs light. The plant uses the glucose for energy and growth, and releases oxygen. Nearly all the oxygen we breathe and the food we eat trace back to this process.", lang: "en", topic: "science" },
  { q: ["What is a cell?","plant cell vs animal cell","explain cells in biology"], a: "A cell is the smallest unit of life. All living things are made of one or more cells.\n\nKey parts in most cells:\n- Cell membrane: controls what goes in and out\n- Nucleus: holds DNA (in plants and animals)\n- Mitochondria: release energy from food\n\nPlant cells also have a rigid cell wall, chloroplasts for photosynthesis, and a large central vacuole. Bacteria are single cells with no nucleus.", lang: "en", topic: "science" },
  { q: ["What is DNA?","explain dna simply","how does dna work"], a: "DNA (deoxyribonucleic acid) is the molecule that carries the instructions for building and running a living thing.\n\nIt's shaped like a twisted ladder, the double helix. The rungs are pairs of four chemical bases, A, T, C and G, where A pairs with T and C pairs with G. The order of these bases forms genes, which tell cells how to make proteins.\n\nYou inherit half your DNA from each biological parent.", lang: "en", topic: "science" },
  { q: ["How does the immune system work?","how does your body fight infections","white blood cells antibodies explained"], a: "Your immune system defends against germs in layers:\n\n1. Barriers: skin, mucus and stomach acid keep many germs out.\n2. Innate response: white blood cells attack invaders quickly and cause inflammation, such as fever or swelling.\n3. Adaptive response: specialized cells (B and T cells) learn to recognize a specific germ. B cells make antibodies that tag it for destruction.\n\nAfterward, memory cells remember the germ, so your body responds faster next time.", lang: "en", topic: "science" },
  { q: ["How do vaccines work?","how does a vaccine train your immune system","vaccines science explained"], a: "A vaccine trains your immune system to recognize a germ without you having to get sick from it.\n\nIt shows your body a harmless piece or version of the germ, such as a weakened or inactivated virus, a protein, or instructions (like mRNA) for making one protein. Your immune system makes antibodies and memory cells. If you later meet the real germ, your body can respond quickly. For advice about your own vaccines, ask a doctor or pharmacist.", lang: "en", topic: "science" },
  { q: ["Why don't antibiotics work on colds?","antibiotics vs viruses","can antibiotics treat the flu"], a: "Antibiotics kill bacteria or stop them from growing. Colds and the flu are caused by viruses, which work very differently, so antibiotics don't help with them.\n\nTaking antibiotics when they aren't needed can cause side effects and helps bacteria become resistant, making future infections harder to treat. Some viral illnesses have antiviral medications instead. A doctor, nurse practitioner or pharmacist can tell you what's appropriate.", lang: "en", topic: "science" },
  { q: ["What is evolution?","explain natural selection","how does evolution work"], a: "Evolution is the change in inherited traits of populations over many generations. Its main driver is natural selection, described by Charles Darwin and Alfred Russel Wallace:\n\n1. Individuals vary, partly because of genetic differences.\n2. Some variations help survival or reproduction in a given environment.\n3. Those individuals tend to leave more offspring.\n4. Over time, helpful traits become more common.\n\nExample: arctic hares and ptarmigans turn white in winter, which helps them hide in snow.", lang: "en", topic: "science" },
  { q: ["Why do we have seasons?","what causes summer and winter","is it because earth is closer to the sun in summer"], a: "Seasons are caused by Earth's tilt, about 23.5°, not by distance from the Sun. Earth is actually closest to the Sun in early January.\n\nWhen the Northern Hemisphere tilts toward the Sun, sunlight hits it more directly and days are longer, giving us summer. When it tilts away, sunlight is more spread out and days are shorter, giving us winter. That's why the Southern Hemisphere has opposite seasons.", lang: "en", topic: "science" },
  { q: ["What causes the northern lights?","aurora borealis explained","why can you see the northern lights in canada"], a: "The northern lights (aurora borealis) happen when charged particles from the Sun, carried by the solar wind, are funnelled by Earth's magnetic field toward the polar regions. They collide with gases high in the atmosphere, making them glow.\n\n- Oxygen usually glows green, sometimes red.\n- Nitrogen can add blue or purple.\n\nMuch of Canada sits under the auroral zone, especially Yukon, the Northwest Territories and Nunavut. Dark, clear nights away from city lights are best.", lang: "en", topic: "science" },
  { q: ["What causes tides?","how do tides work","why are the tides so big in the bay of fundy"], a: "Tides are caused mainly by the Moon's gravity, with help from the Sun. The Moon pulls on Earth's oceans, creating bulges of water on the side facing it and on the opposite side. As Earth rotates through these bulges, most coasts get two high tides and two low tides about every day.\n\nThe Bay of Fundy, between New Brunswick and Nova Scotia, has some of the highest tides in the world because its shape funnels and amplifies the water.", lang: "en", topic: "science" },
  { q: ["What's the difference between weather and climate?","climate vs weather","if it's cold today does that mean climate change isn't real"], a: "Weather is what's happening in the atmosphere right now or over a few days, like today's snowstorm or tomorrow's high.\n\nClimate is the long-term pattern of weather in a place, usually averaged over 30 years or more.\n\nA saying that sums it up: \"Climate is what you expect; weather is what you get.\" One cold day doesn't tell you about climate; trends over decades do.", lang: "en", topic: "science" },
  { q: ["What is the greenhouse effect?","how does the greenhouse effect work","why does co2 warm the planet"], a: "The greenhouse effect is how certain gases keep Earth warm. Sunlight warms the surface, which gives off heat as infrared radiation. Greenhouse gases such as water vapour, carbon dioxide and methane absorb some of that heat and send part of it back toward the surface.\n\nThis natural effect makes Earth liveable. Adding more of these gases, mainly by burning fossil fuels, traps more heat and warms the climate.", lang: "en", topic: "science" },
  { q: ["What is plate tectonics?","how do earthquakes happen","why does bc get earthquakes"], a: "Earth's outer shell is broken into large plates that move very slowly, a few centimetres a year, about as fast as fingernails grow. Where plates meet, they can pull apart, collide or slide past each other.\n\nEarthquakes happen when stress builds along a fault and is suddenly released. Volcanoes and mountain ranges also form at plate boundaries. British Columbia's coast is near the Cascadia subduction zone, which is why that region has higher earthquake risk.", lang: "en", topic: "science" },
  { q: ["How does the water cycle work?","explain the water cycle","where does rain come from"], a: "The water cycle is the continuous movement of water around Earth:\n\n1. Evaporation: the Sun heats water in oceans, lakes and rivers, turning it into vapour. Plants release vapour too.\n2. Condensation: the vapour cools as it rises and forms clouds.\n3. Precipitation: water falls as rain, snow or hail.\n4. Collection: it flows into rivers, lakes, oceans and groundwater.\n\nThen the cycle repeats. The total amount of water on Earth stays roughly the same.", lang: "en", topic: "science" },
  { q: ["Why does the moon have phases?","moon phases explained","why does the moon change shape"], a: "The Moon doesn't make its own light; it reflects sunlight. Half of it is always lit by the Sun, but as it orbits Earth (about every 29.5 days), we see different amounts of that lit half.\n\nThe phases go: new moon → waxing crescent → first quarter → waxing gibbous → full moon → waning gibbous → last quarter → waning crescent.\n\nThe phases are not caused by Earth's shadow; that only happens during a lunar eclipse.", lang: "en", topic: "science" },
  { q: ["Why do leaves change colour in the fall?","why do maple leaves turn red","autumn leaves science"], a: "In summer, leaves are green because of chlorophyll, which they use for photosynthesis. As days shorten and temperatures drop in fall, trees stop making chlorophyll and it breaks down.\n\n- Yellows and oranges (carotenoids) were there all along, hidden by the green.\n- Reds and purples (anthocyanins) are made fresh in fall, especially in maples, and are boosted by sunny days and cool nights.\n\nThen trees seal off the leaves, which fall.", lang: "en", topic: "science" },
  { q: ["What is wind chill?","why does wind make it feel colder","what does feels like -30 mean"], a: "Wind chill describes how cold it feels on exposed skin when wind is combined with low temperatures. Your body warms a thin layer of air next to your skin; wind blows that layer away, so you lose heat faster.\n\nEnvironment Canada reports wind chill as a \"feels like\" temperature. It doesn't make objects colder than the air temperature, but it does raise the risk of frostbite on skin. Cover exposed skin when wind chill values are very low.", lang: "en", topic: "science" },
  { q: ["What is a black hole?","explain black holes simply","how do black holes form"], a: "A black hole is a region of space where gravity is so strong that nothing, not even light, can escape once it crosses the boundary called the event horizon.\n\nMany form when a very massive star runs out of fuel and its core collapses. Supermassive black holes, millions or billions of times the Sun's mass, sit at the centres of most large galaxies, including our Milky Way. Scientists detect them by their effects on nearby stars, gas and light.", lang: "en", topic: "science" },
  { q: ["How does sound travel?","can sound travel in space","why does sound move faster in water"], a: "Sound is a vibration that travels as a wave through a material, such as air, water or solids, by making particles bump into their neighbours.\n\n- In air it moves at about 343 m/s at room temperature.\n- In water it's about four times faster, and faster still in steel, because particles are closer together and more tightly linked.\n- In space there's almost no material, so sound can't travel. Explosions in space would be silent.", lang: "en", topic: "science" },
  { q: ["Who runs education in Canada?","is there a national education system in canada","does the federal government control schools"], a: "Education in Canada is run by the provinces and territories, not the federal government. Each one has its own ministry or department of education that sets curriculum, graduation requirements and school calendars.\n\nThat's why things like grade structure, school start ages and diploma names differ across the country. Local school boards (in Quebec, school service centres in most cases) manage day-to-day operations. For your area, check your provincial ministry of education.", lang: "en", topic: "education" },
  { q: ["How does the school system work in Canada?","elementary vs secondary school canada","what grades are high school in canada"], a: "Most provinces split schooling into:\n- Kindergarten (often starting around age 4 or 5)\n- Elementary school (roughly grades 1 to 6 or 8)\n- Secondary or high school (roughly grades 9 to 12)\n\nSome provinces also have middle or junior high schools. Quebec is different: secondary school ends after Secondary V (equivalent to grade 11), followed by CEGEP. Public schooling is free for residents, and attendance is mandatory until a certain age, which varies by province.", lang: "en", topic: "education" },
  { q: ["What is CEGEP?","how does cegep work in quebec","do i need cegep to go to university in quebec"], a: "CEGEP (collège d'enseignement général et professionnel) is a level of education unique to Quebec, taken after secondary school.\n\nThere are two main tracks:\n- Pre-university programs, usually 2 years, which are generally required to enter a Quebec university\n- Technical programs, usually 3 years, which lead directly to a career but can also lead to university\n\nPublic CEGEPs are tuition-free for Quebec residents, though other fees apply. Students from outside Quebec may enter Quebec universities through different admission paths.", lang: "en", topic: "education" },
  { q: ["What's the difference between college and university in Canada?","college vs university canada","should i go to college or university"], a: "In Canada, the words mean different things than in the US:\n\n- College: usually practical, career-focused programs (diplomas, certificates, apprenticeships, some applied degrees). Programs are often shorter, with hands-on training and co-op placements.\n- University: academic and professional degrees (bachelor's, master's, PhD), with more theory and research.\n\nBoth can lead to good careers. Many colleges and universities have transfer agreements, so you can start at one and move to the other.", lang: "en", topic: "education" },
  { q: ["How do I apply to university in Canada?","university application process canada","how to apply for uni in ontario"], a: "The general process:\n1. Research programs and their admission requirements (specific high school courses and grades).\n2. Apply by the deadline. Some provinces use a central service. Ontario universities use OUAC, for example, while others accept applications directly.\n3. Send transcripts and any extra materials (essays, portfolios, reference letters).\n4. Watch for offers and accept by the reply date.\n\nApply early for scholarships and residence. Each school's admissions office can confirm deadlines and fees, which change yearly.", lang: "en", topic: "education" },
  { q: ["How do I apply to college in Canada?","applying to college ontario","college admissions process"], a: "Steps are similar across Canada:\n1. Pick programs and check requirements; some need specific high school courses, a portfolio or an interview.\n2. Apply. Ontario colleges use a central service called OntarioColleges; elsewhere you often apply directly to the college. In Quebec, CEGEP applications go through regional services such as SRAM.\n3. Submit transcripts and any required documents.\n4. Accept your offer and pay the deposit by the deadline.\n\nPopular programs fill quickly, so apply early.", lang: "en", topic: "education" },
  { q: ["What is OSAP?","how does student aid work in canada","can i get a student loan for college"], a: "OSAP is Ontario's student financial aid program. Every province and territory has its own version, such as StudentAid BC, Alberta Student Aid or Quebec's Aide financière aux études, usually combined with federal Canada Student Grants and Loans.\n\nAid is mainly based on financial need, considering your income, family situation and costs. It can include grants (which you don't repay) and loans (which you do). Amounts and rules change, so check your province's student aid website or canada.ca.", lang: "en", topic: "education" },
  { q: ["Can I get my foreign degree recognized in Canada?","credential recognition canada","how do i get my diploma evaluated for canada"], a: "Yes, but it depends on your goal:\n- For immigration or general employment: an Educational Credential Assessment (ECA) from a designated organization compares your education to Canadian standards.\n- For regulated professions (nursing, engineering, teaching, law, medicine and others): you must be licensed by the provincial regulatory body, which may require exams, extra training or supervised work.\n- For further study: the school's admissions office evaluates your credentials.\n\nStart with canada.ca or your province's regulator.", lang: "en", topic: "education" },
  { q: ["What are LINC classes?","free english classes for newcomers canada","where can i learn english as an immigrant"], a: "LINC (Language Instruction for Newcomers to Canada) offers free English classes, funded by the federal government, for eligible permanent residents and some other newcomers, outside Quebec. Some areas also offer French classes under a similar program.\n\nYou usually start with a language assessment to place you at the right level, measured by the Canadian Language Benchmarks. Many classes include child-minding or online options. Local settlement agencies can help you register.", lang: "en", topic: "education" },
  { q: ["What is francisation in Quebec?","free french classes quebec immigrants","how to learn french in quebec"], a: "Francisation refers to French-language courses Quebec offers to immigrants and other eligible people to help them integrate, work and study in French.\n\nCourses come in several formats: full-time, part-time, online, and in workplaces. Eligible students may receive financial assistance in some cases. The program is managed by the province, now largely through Francisation Québec. Eligibility and details change, so check the Quebec government's website or a local community organization.", lang: "en", topic: "education" },
  { q: ["What's the best way to study for exams?","study tips that actually work","how do i study better"], a: "Techniques backed by research:\n- Active recall: test yourself instead of rereading. Use flashcards or practice questions.\n- Spaced repetition: review over several days rather than cramming the night before.\n- Interleaving: mix different types of problems.\n- Teach it: explaining a topic out loud reveals gaps.\n- Short focused sessions: try 25 to 50 minutes, then a short break.\n- Sleep: memory consolidates while you sleep, so an all-nighter usually backfires.", lang: "en", topic: "education" },
  { q: ["How do I stop procrastinating on homework?","cant focus on studying","tips to stop procrastinating"], a: "Try these:\n- Make the first step tiny: \"open the document and write one sentence.\"\n- Break big tasks into chunks with mini-deadlines.\n- Use a timer: work 25 minutes, break 5 (the Pomodoro technique).\n- Put your phone in another room or use focus mode.\n- Work in the same place each time so your brain links it with studying.\n- Reward yourself after finishing a chunk.\n\nIf focus problems are ongoing, your school's student services can help.", lang: "en", topic: "education" },
  { q: ["How do I write a good essay?","essay writing tips","how to structure an essay"], a: "A clear structure goes a long way:\n1. Introduction: hook the reader, give context, and end with a thesis, your main argument in one or two sentences.\n2. Body paragraphs: one main point each. Start with a topic sentence, support it with evidence, and explain how it connects to your thesis.\n3. Conclusion: restate the thesis in new words and explain why it matters. Don't add new evidence.\n\nOutline first, write a rough draft, then revise.", lang: "en", topic: "education" },
  { q: ["How do I write a strong thesis statement?","what is a thesis statement","thesis statement example"], a: "A thesis statement is a one- or two-sentence claim that your whole essay supports. It should be specific, arguable and focused.\n\nWeak: \"Climate change is a problem.\"\nStronger: \"Canadian cities should expand public transit because it cuts emissions, reduces traffic and improves access for low-income residents.\"\n\nThe stronger version takes a position and previews the main points. Put it at the end of your introduction.", lang: "en", topic: "education" },
  { q: ["Why do I need to cite sources?","what is citing sources","apa vs mla citation"], a: "Citing sources gives credit to others' ideas, lets readers check your evidence, and avoids plagiarism, which schools treat seriously.\n\nCite whenever you quote, paraphrase or use specific facts or ideas from someone else. Common styles:\n- APA: social sciences, education, psychology\n- MLA: English, humanities\n- Chicago: history and some other fields\n\nEach style has in-text citations and a reference list. Ask your instructor which one to use; reference managers like Zotero can help.", lang: "en", topic: "education" },
  { q: ["What counts as plagiarism?","is paraphrasing plagiarism","can i get in trouble for copying from the internet"], a: "Plagiarism is presenting someone else's words or ideas as your own. It includes:\n- Copying text without quotation marks and a citation\n- Paraphrasing without citing the source\n- Submitting work written by someone else\n- Reusing your own old assignment without permission\n- Using AI tools in ways your course doesn't allow\n\nParaphrasing is fine if you put it in your own words and cite it. Consequences can range from a zero to suspension, so check your school's academic integrity policy.", lang: "en", topic: "education" },
  { q: ["What is a co-op program?","co-op vs internship canada","is co-op worth it"], a: "A co-op program alternates academic terms with paid work terms related to your field. Many Canadian colleges and universities offer them, and the University of Waterloo is well known for its large co-op program.\n\nBenefits:\n- Real work experience before you graduate\n- Income to help with costs\n- Networking and possible job offers\n\nCo-op can add time to your program. Internships are similar but are usually one-time placements, not alternating terms.", lang: "en", topic: "education" },
  { q: ["How do I enrol my child in school in Canada?","registering kids for school newcomer","how to register my child for public school"], a: "Contact your local public school board (or school service centre in Quebec). Your address usually determines your designated school.\n\nYou'll typically need:\n- Proof of address\n- The child's birth certificate or passport\n- Immigration documents, if applicable\n- Immunization records\n- Previous school records\n\nNewcomer students may be assessed for language support. Public school is free for residents. In Quebec, most children of immigrants attend French-language schools under the Charter of the French Language.", lang: "en", topic: "education" },
  { q: ["Can I choose between English and French school?","french immersion vs french school","what is french immersion"], a: "There are a few options:\n- French immersion: offered by English school boards in many provinces. Most subjects are taught in French, so students become bilingual. It's open to most families.\n- French-language (francophone) schools: designed for children with French-language rights under the Charter of Rights and Freedoms, such as children of French-speaking parents.\n- Quebec: most students must attend French schools, with specific eligibility rules for English schools.\n\nCheck your local school board for availability.", lang: "en", topic: "education" },
  { q: ["What is an RESP?","saving for my kid's education canada","how does an resp work"], a: "An RESP (Registered Education Savings Plan) is a government-registered account for saving toward a child's post-secondary education.\n\nKey ideas:\n- Investment growth is tax-sheltered while in the plan.\n- The federal government adds the Canada Education Savings Grant on contributions, and lower-income families may get the Canada Learning Bond.\n- Some provinces add their own incentives.\n\nGrant amounts and limits can change. For choosing an RESP or investments, speak with a licensed financial advisor.", lang: "en", topic: "education" },
  { q: ["What's the difference between a diploma, a degree and a certificate?","college diploma vs degree canada","credential types canada"], a: "In general:\n- Certificate: short and focused, often one year or less, for a specific skill.\n- Diploma: usually 2 to 3 years at a college, career-oriented with hands-on training.\n- Advanced diploma: typically 3 years at a college.\n- Bachelor's degree: usually 3 to 4 years at a university (some colleges offer applied degrees).\n- Graduate credentials: master's and doctoral degrees after a bachelor's.\n\nNames and lengths vary by province.", lang: "en", topic: "education" },
  { q: ["What is GPA and how does it work in Canada?","gpa scale canada","how do canadian universities grade"], a: "GPA (grade point average) summarizes your grades as one number. In Canada there's no single national scale. Schools may use a 4.0, 4.3, 9 or 12-point scale, or percentages.\n\nIn Quebec, CEGEPs use the R score (cote R) for university admission. When applying, schools usually convert grades themselves. If you're comparing systems, check how each school's transcript legend defines its scale.", lang: "en", topic: "education" },
  { q: ["Can adults go back to high school in Canada?","finish high school as an adult","how to get my high school diploma as an adult"], a: "Yes. Every province offers ways for adults to finish secondary school, often free for residents:\n- Adult education centres or continuing education programs run by school boards\n- Online or distance courses\n- High school equivalency tests, such as the GED in some provinces\n- Prior learning assessment, which may give credit for work or life experience\n\nIn Quebec, this is called formation générale des adultes. Contact your local school board to start.", lang: "en", topic: "education" },
  { q: ["What is an apprenticeship in Canada?","how to become an electrician apprentice","skilled trades training canada"], a: "An apprenticeship combines paid on-the-job training with a journeyperson and shorter classroom sessions, usually at a college. It's the main path into skilled trades like electrician, plumber, carpenter and welder.\n\nApprenticeships are managed by each province. Many trades offer the Red Seal endorsement, an interprovincial standard that makes it easier to work across Canada. Your first step is usually finding an employer sponsor and registering with your province's apprenticeship authority.", lang: "en", topic: "education" },
  { q: ["How can I take notes better in class?","best note taking methods","cornell notes explained"], a: "Good note-taking is about processing, not copying everything.\n\nMethods:\n- Cornell method: divide the page into notes, a cue column for questions or keywords, and a summary at the bottom.\n- Outlining: headings and indented sub-points.\n- Mind maps: good for connecting ideas.\n\nTips: write in your own words, mark things you don't understand, and review within 24 hours. Handwriting may help some people remember more than typing word for word.", lang: "en", topic: "education" },
  { q: ["How do I write a research paper?","research paper steps","how to start a research assignment"], a: "A step-by-step approach:\n1. Choose and narrow your topic into a clear research question.\n2. Find credible sources through your library's databases, academic journals and reputable organizations.\n3. Take notes and record citation details as you go.\n4. Write a thesis that answers your question.\n5. Outline, then draft.\n6. Cite every source in the required style.\n7. Revise for argument and clarity, then proofread.\n\nYour school library can help you find good sources.", lang: "en", topic: "education" },
  { q: ["How can I tell if a source is reliable?","how to check if a website is credible","evaluating sources for school"], a: "Ask these questions:\n- Who wrote it? Look for qualified authors or reputable organizations.\n- Where is it published? Peer-reviewed journals, government sites and established news outlets are generally stronger.\n- When? Check whether the information is current enough.\n- Why? Is it informing, selling or persuading?\n- Is there evidence? Good sources cite data and other sources.\n\nCross-check important facts with more than one source.", lang: "en", topic: "education" },
  { q: ["What's the best way to learn English fast?","tips for improving my english","how to practise english every day"], a: "Consistency beats intensity. Try:\n- Daily listening: podcasts, radio or shows with subtitles\n- Speaking practice: conversation circles at libraries or settlement agencies\n- Reading at your level: graded readers or simple news\n- Writing: short daily journal entries\n- Vocabulary: learn words in context and review with spaced-repetition flashcards\n\nIn Canada, free classes such as LINC may be available if you're eligible, and many public libraries run free conversation groups.", lang: "en", topic: "education" },
  { q: ["Is university free in Canada?","how much does university cost in canada","tuition fees canada"], a: "No, post-secondary tuition isn't free in most of Canada. Fees vary by province, school, program and whether you're a domestic or international student, and they change from year to year.\n\nIn Quebec, public CEGEPs are tuition-free for residents, though other fees apply. Financial aid, scholarships and bursaries can help reduce costs. I can look up current tuition for a specific school if you'd like.", lang: "en", topic: "education" },
  { q: ["Can I study in Canada as an international student?","study permit canada","how do international students come to canada"], a: "Yes. Most international students need a study permit for programs longer than six months. The general steps:\n1. Get accepted by a designated learning institution (DLI).\n2. Apply for a study permit through Immigration, Refugees and Citizenship Canada (IRCC).\n3. Show proof of funds and meet other requirements.\n\nRules, caps and processing times change often, so check canada.ca for current requirements.", lang: "en", topic: "education" },
  { q: ["How should a Canadian resume look?","canadian resume format","what goes on a resume in canada"], a: "A Canadian resume is usually 1 to 2 pages and includes:\n- Name, phone, email, city/province, and optionally LinkedIn\n- A short professional summary\n- Work experience, most recent first, with achievements\n- Education and certifications\n- Relevant skills, including languages\n\nLeave out your photo, age, date of birth, marital status, religion, SIN and references (\"available on request\" is optional). Tailor it to each job using keywords from the posting.", lang: "en", topic: "jobs" },
  { q: ["Should I put a photo on my resume in Canada?","do canadian resumes have pictures","include age and marital status on resume?"], a: "No. Canadian resumes normally don't include a photo, age, date of birth, marital status, nationality, religion or health information. Human rights laws restrict employers from making hiring decisions based on these personal characteristics, so including them can work against you.\n\nFocus on skills, experience and results instead. A professional photo on your LinkedIn profile is fine.", lang: "en", topic: "jobs" },
  { q: ["How do I write a cover letter?","cover letter tips canada","do i need a cover letter"], a: "A cover letter is still worth sending unless the posting says not to. Keep it to one page:\n1. Opening: the role you're applying for and a strong reason you're a fit.\n2. Middle: 1 or 2 short paragraphs connecting your experience to their needs, with specific results.\n3. Closing: thank them and express interest in an interview.\n\nAddress a person by name if you can find it, and customize each letter. A generic letter is easy to spot.", lang: "en", topic: "jobs" },
  { q: ["How do I make my resume pass applicant tracking systems?","ats friendly resume","my resume isn't getting any calls"], a: "Many employers use applicant tracking systems (ATS) to screen resumes. To improve your chances:\n- Use keywords and job titles from the posting, where they're true for you\n- Use a simple layout with standard headings like \"Experience\" and \"Education\"\n- Avoid tables, text boxes, images and unusual fonts\n- Submit a .docx or PDF, as requested\n- Quantify results: \"cut processing time by 20%\" beats \"improved processes\"\n\nAlso ask someone to review it for clarity.", lang: "en", topic: "jobs" },
  { q: ["What is Canadian experience and why do employers want it?","no canadian experience how to get a job","newcomer can't get hired without canadian experience"], a: "\"Canadian experience\" usually means employers want confidence in your communication style, workplace norms and knowledge of local standards. Ways to build it:\n- Volunteer in your field or community\n- Bridging programs for internationally trained professionals\n- Short courses or certifications recognized in Canada\n- Contract, temporary or part-time work\n- Mentorship and networking programs through settlement agencies\n\nIn some provinces, such as Ontario, regulators can't require Canadian experience for professional licensing.", lang: "en", topic: "jobs" },
  { q: ["How do I prepare for a job interview?","job interview tips","interview tomorrow what should i do"], a: "Prepare in three areas:\n1. Research: the company's services, values and the role's main duties.\n2. Stories: prepare 5 or 6 examples using the STAR method (Situation, Task, Action, Result) for common questions about teamwork, conflict, challenges and achievements.\n3. Logistics: confirm the time, location or video link, and test your tech.\n\nOn the day: arrive 10 minutes early, dress a bit more formally than the workplace, and prepare 2 or 3 questions to ask them.", lang: "en", topic: "jobs" },
  { q: ["What is the STAR method?","how to answer behavioural interview questions","tell me about a time when questions how to answer"], a: "STAR is a way to answer behavioural questions like \"Tell me about a time when...\" clearly:\n- Situation: brief context\n- Task: what you needed to achieve\n- Action: what you specifically did (say \"I,\" not just \"we\")\n- Result: the outcome, with numbers if possible, and what you learned\n\nExample result: \"We finished two days early and the client renewed.\" Aim for 1 to 2 minutes per answer.", lang: "en", topic: "jobs" },
  { q: ["How do I answer 'tell me about yourself'?","tell me about yourself interview answer","how to introduce myself in an interview"], a: "Use a short present-past-future structure, about one minute:\n1. Present: your current role or focus and a key strength.\n2. Past: relevant experience or achievements that led you here.\n3. Future: why this role and company are your next step.\n\nKeep it professional and relevant to the job; skip your full life story and personal details. Practise out loud so it sounds natural, not memorized.", lang: "en", topic: "jobs" },
  { q: ["What questions are illegal in a Canadian job interview?","can an employer ask my age in an interview","interviewer asked if i have kids is that allowed"], a: "Under human rights laws, employers generally shouldn't ask about protected grounds unless there's a genuine job requirement. Examples:\n- Age or date of birth\n- Marital or family status, pregnancy or plans for children\n- Religion, ethnicity or place of origin\n- Disability or health conditions\n- Sexual orientation or gender identity\n\nThey may ask whether you're legally entitled to work in Canada. If you face discrimination, contact your provincial human rights commission or tribunal.", lang: "en", topic: "jobs" },
  { q: ["What questions should I ask at the end of an interview?","questions to ask the interviewer","do you have any questions for us what do i say"], a: "Always have a few ready. Good options:\n- \"What would success look like in the first 90 days?\"\n- \"What are the biggest challenges for this role right now?\"\n- \"How would you describe the team and how it works together?\"\n- \"What opportunities are there for learning and growth?\"\n- \"What are the next steps in the hiring process?\"\n\nSave salary questions for later stages unless they bring it up.", lang: "en", topic: "jobs" },
  { q: ["Should I send a thank-you email after an interview?","follow up after interview","how long to wait to hear back after interview"], a: "Yes. Send a short thank-you email within 24 hours. Thank them for their time, mention something specific from the conversation, and briefly restate your interest.\n\nIf they gave a timeline and it passes, a polite follow-up a few days later is fine. If not, waiting about a week is reasonable. Keep it brief and professional, and keep applying elsewhere in the meantime.", lang: "en", topic: "jobs" },
  { q: ["What is Job Bank?","where to find jobs in canada","best job search sites canada"], a: "Job Bank is the Government of Canada's free job search service. You can search postings, explore wages and job outlooks by occupation and region, and set up job alerts.\n\nOther common sources:\n- LinkedIn and general job boards\n- Company career pages\n- Provincial employment services and settlement agencies\n- Staffing agencies for contract or temporary work\n- Your own network, since many jobs are filled through referrals", lang: "en", topic: "jobs" },
  { q: ["How do I network in Canada?","networking tips for newcomers","how to network when you don't know anyone"], a: "Networking in Canada is usually friendly and low-pressure. Ways to start:\n- Informational interviews: ask professionals for 15 to 20 minutes to learn about their work. Don't ask for a job directly.\n- Join professional associations and attend meetups or industry events.\n- Volunteer, which builds both contacts and experience.\n- Use LinkedIn: personalize connection requests and engage with posts.\n- Mentorship programs through settlement agencies.\n\nFollow up and thank people. Relationships build over time.", lang: "en", topic: "jobs" },
  { q: ["How do I make a good LinkedIn profile?","linkedin tips","how to improve my linkedin"], a: "Key steps:\n- Professional headshot and a simple background banner\n- A headline that says what you do or want, not just \"Seeking opportunities\"\n- An About section of a few short paragraphs on your strengths and goals\n- Experience with achievements, not just duties\n- Skills relevant to your target jobs\n- A custom URL and your location set correctly\n\nTurn on \"Open to work\" (it can be visible to recruiters only) and engage regularly with your field.", lang: "en", topic: "jobs" },
  { q: ["What is Canadian workplace culture like?","canadian work culture tips","how do people act at work in canada"], a: "Common features of Canadian workplaces:\n- Politeness and indirect feedback: criticism is often softened, so listen carefully.\n- Punctuality: be on time for meetings.\n- Teamwork: collaboration and consensus are valued.\n- Relatively flat hierarchy: first names are common, even with managers.\n- Work-life balance: personal time is generally respected.\n- Small talk: weather, weekends and sports are safe topics.\n\nCultures vary by industry, region and company, so observe and ask colleagues.", lang: "en", topic: "jobs" },
  { q: ["Who sets employment rules in Canada?","are labour laws federal or provincial","employment standards canada"], a: "Most workplaces are covered by provincial or territorial employment standards laws, which set rules on minimum wage, hours, overtime, vacation, public holidays and termination notice.\n\nA smaller group of workers are in federally regulated industries, such as banks, airlines, interprovincial transport and telecommunications, and are covered by the Canada Labour Code instead.\n\nRules differ by jurisdiction, so check your province's employment standards office for details.", lang: "en", topic: "jobs" },
  { q: ["What is the minimum wage in Canada?","minimum wage ontario","how much is min wage"], a: "Minimum wage in Canada is set by each province and territory, and there's also a federal minimum wage for federally regulated workplaces. The amounts are adjusted regularly, often yearly, and some provinces have different rates for certain jobs.\n\nI don't want to give you an outdated number. I can look up the current rate for your province, or you can check your province's employment standards website.", lang: "en", topic: "jobs" },
  { q: ["What is a Record of Employment?","what's an roe","my employer hasn't issued my ROE"], a: "A Record of Employment (ROE) is a form employers issue when you have an interruption of earnings, for example if you're laid off, quit, are dismissed, or go on parental or sick leave.\n\nService Canada uses it to decide whether you qualify for Employment Insurance (EI) and how much you'd receive. Most employers submit it electronically, and you can view it in your My Service Canada Account. If your employer doesn't issue it, contact Service Canada.", lang: "en", topic: "jobs" },
  { q: ["How does Employment Insurance work?","what is ei in canada","can i get ei if i get laid off"], a: "Employment Insurance (EI) is a federal program that provides temporary income support to eligible workers. Main types:\n- Regular benefits: if you lose your job through no fault of your own, like a layoff\n- Sickness, maternity and parental, and caregiving benefits\n\nYou generally need enough insurable hours in a recent period, and you should apply as soon as you stop working. Quitting without just cause or being fired for misconduct can affect eligibility. Amounts and rules change; check canada.ca.", lang: "en", topic: "jobs" },
  { q: ["What is a T4 slip?","when do i get my t4","what's a t4 for taxes"], a: "A T4 is a tax slip your employer gives you summarizing your employment income for the year and the deductions taken, such as income tax, CPP or QPP contributions, and EI premiums.\n\nEmployers must issue T4s by the end of February for the previous year. You use it to file your income tax return, and the CRA also gets a copy. Quebec workers also receive an RL-1 slip for their provincial return. Your T4 is usually available in CRA My Account too.", lang: "en", topic: "jobs" },
  { q: ["What is a SIN and do I need one to work?","social insurance number","how to get a sin number"], a: "A Social Insurance Number (SIN) is a 9-digit number you need to work in Canada and to access government programs and benefits. You apply through Service Canada, online, in person or by mail, with proof of identity and status.\n\nKeep it private. Give it only when required, such as to your employer after you're hired, to your bank for interest income, or to the government. Don't put it on your resume or share it with unverified callers.", lang: "en", topic: "jobs" },
  { q: ["How do unions work in Canada?","should i join a union","what does a union do"], a: "A union represents a group of workers in negotiating with the employer. Key ideas:\n- Collective agreement: a contract covering wages, hours, benefits and working conditions.\n- Union dues: members pay dues, usually deducted from pay.\n- Grievances: the union can help if the employer breaks the agreement.\n- Certification: workers can organize a union through a process set by labour law, mostly provincial.\n\nIf your workplace is unionized, your job is usually covered by the collective agreement automatically.", lang: "en", topic: "jobs" },
  { q: ["What are my basic rights at work in Canada?","workplace rights canada","can my boss make me work without pay"], a: "In general, workers in Canada have the right to:\n- Be paid at least minimum wage for all hours worked\n- Rest periods, vacation and public holidays as set by law\n- A safe workplace, including the right to refuse unsafe work\n- Freedom from discrimination and harassment\n- Notice or pay in lieu when dismissed without cause, in most cases\n\nDetails vary by province. If you think your rights are being violated, contact your province's employment standards office.", lang: "en", topic: "jobs" },
  { q: ["Can I refuse unsafe work?","right to refuse unsafe work canada","my job is dangerous what can i do"], a: "Yes. Workers across Canada have the right to refuse work they reasonably believe is dangerous. Generally:\n1. Tell your supervisor right away and explain why.\n2. The employer must investigate, often with a health and safety committee member or representative.\n3. If it's not resolved, a government health and safety inspector can be called.\n\nYour employer can't punish you for using this right in good faith. Procedures vary by province or federal jurisdiction.", lang: "en", topic: "jobs" },
  { q: ["What should I do if I'm harassed at work?","workplace harassment canada","my manager keeps bullying me"], a: "Steps you can take:\n1. Write down each incident: date, time, what happened, witnesses.\n2. Check your workplace's harassment policy; most employers are required to have one.\n3. Report it to your supervisor, HR or another designated person.\n4. If that doesn't work, contact your provincial employment standards or health and safety office, or human rights commission if discrimination is involved.\n5. A union representative or employment lawyer can advise you.\n\nIf you're in danger, call 911.", lang: "en", topic: "jobs" },
  { q: ["How do I negotiate my salary?","salary negotiation tips","should i accept the first offer"], a: "Tips for negotiating:\n- Research the market range for your role and region; Job Bank lists wage data by occupation.\n- Let them make the first offer if possible.\n- Thank them, then ask for time to review.\n- Counter with a specific number backed by your skills and research.\n- Consider the whole package: vacation, flexible work, training and benefits.\n- Stay positive and collaborative.\n\nMost employers expect some negotiation, and a polite request rarely costs you the offer.", lang: "en", topic: "jobs" },
  { q: ["What should I say when asked about salary expectations?","salary expectations question","how to answer what salary do you want"], a: "Give a researched range rather than a single number, with the bottom of the range at a figure you'd genuinely accept. For example: \"Based on my research and experience, I'm looking for something in the range of X to Y, depending on the full compensation package.\"\n\nResearch the role using job postings, Job Bank wage data and contacts in the field. Note that some provinces now require salary ranges in job postings, which can help.", lang: "en", topic: "jobs" },
  { q: ["How do I resign from my job professionally?","how to quit my job","how much notice to give when quitting"], a: "Steps to resign well:\n1. Tell your manager in person or by video first.\n2. Follow up with a short written resignation letter with your last day.\n3. Give proper notice; two weeks is common in Canada, but check your contract and provincial rules.\n4. Offer to help with the transition and document your work.\n5. Stay positive, since references matter.\n\nYour employer must issue a Record of Employment, and you should receive final pay including owed vacation pay.", lang: "en", topic: "jobs" },
  { q: ["What happens if I get fired in Canada?","terminated without cause what am i owed","laid off what are my rights"], a: "If you're dismissed without cause, employers in most jurisdictions must give written notice or pay in lieu of notice, based on your length of service. Some workers may also be owed severance pay depending on provincial rules, and common law may provide more than the minimum.\n\nYou should also receive your final pay, vacation pay owed, and a Record of Employment. For your specific situation, contact your employment standards office or an employment lawyer.", lang: "en", topic: "jobs" },
  { q: ["What's the difference between an employee and a contractor?","independent contractor vs employee canada","my boss says i'm a contractor but i work full time"], a: "Employees work under the employer's control, use its tools and schedule, and are covered by employment standards. The employer deducts tax, CPP or QPP, and EI.\n\nIndependent contractors run their own business, control how they work, can work for multiple clients, and handle their own taxes.\n\nWhat matters is the actual relationship, not the label. If you think you're misclassified, the CRA can issue a ruling, and your provincial employment standards office can help.", lang: "en", topic: "jobs" },
  { q: ["Do I get paid for public holidays?","stat holiday pay canada","what are statutory holidays"], a: "Most workers in Canada are entitled to public (statutory) holidays with pay, but the list of holidays and eligibility rules depend on your province or territory, or the Canada Labour Code if you're federally regulated.\n\nCommon ones include New Year's Day, Good Friday, Canada Day, Labour Day and Christmas Day. If you work on a public holiday, you may get premium pay or a substitute day off. Check your province's employment standards for specifics.", lang: "en", topic: "jobs" },
  { q: ["How do references work in Canada?","who should i use as a reference","do canadian employers check references"], a: "Yes, many Canadian employers check references, usually near the end of the hiring process.\n\nTips:\n- Choose 2 or 3 people who know your work well, such as former supervisors, managers or instructors.\n- Ask permission first and tell them about the job.\n- Prepare a separate reference list; don't put it on your resume.\n- Include their name, title, organization, phone and email.\n\nIf you're new to Canada, volunteer supervisors or instructors can be good references.", lang: "en", topic: "jobs" },
  { q: ["How do I write a professional email to an employer?","professional email tips","how to email a recruiter"], a: "Keep it clear and short:\n- Subject line: specific, like \"Application: Marketing Coordinator\"\n- Greeting: \"Hello Ms. Tremblay\" or \"Hi Alex\" if they use first names\n- First line: who you are and why you're writing\n- Body: 2 or 3 short paragraphs or bullet points\n- Close: a clear next step and \"Thank you\" or \"Best regards\"\n- Signature: full name, phone, LinkedIn\n\nProofread names and attachments before sending.", lang: "en", topic: "jobs" },
  { q: ["How can I spot a job scam?","fake job offer red flags","they want me to buy equipment before starting work"], a: "Red flags for job scams:\n- A job offer without an interview\n- Pay that seems too good for little work\n- Requests to pay for training, equipment or a \"starter kit\"\n- Asking you to deposit a cheque and send money back\n- Requests for your SIN or banking info before a real offer\n- Messages from personal email addresses or only via text or chat apps\n\nVerify the company independently. Report scams to the Canadian Anti-Fraud Centre.", lang: "en", topic: "jobs" },
  { q: ["How do I explain a gap in my resume?","employment gap resume","i haven't worked in 2 years how do i explain it"], a: "Be honest, brief and positive. Gaps are common, for reasons like caregiving, health, immigration, studies or layoffs.\n\nTips:\n- On your resume, you can use years only and add a one-line note, such as \"Career break: relocation to Canada.\"\n- In interviews, explain in one or two sentences, then focus on what you learned or did, like courses or volunteering, and why you're ready now.\n- You don't need to share private health or family details.", lang: "en", topic: "jobs" },
  { q: ["How do driver's licences work in Canada?","who issues drivers licences in canada","is a drivers licence federal or provincial"], a: "Driver's licences are issued by each province and territory, not the federal government. Each has its own rules, tests and fees.\n\nMost use graduated licensing, where new drivers move through stages with restrictions before getting a full licence. You get your licence through your provincial licensing office, such as ServiceOntario, ICBC in BC, a registry agent in Alberta, or the SAAQ in Quebec. A licence from one province is valid when visiting others, but you must switch if you move.", lang: "en", topic: "transport" },
  { q: ["What is graduated licensing?","g1 g2 g explained","how does the learner licence work"], a: "Graduated licensing lets new drivers gain experience in stages with fewer restrictions as they progress. Examples:\n- Ontario: G1 (learner, must drive with an experienced driver), G2 (probationary), then full G\n- BC: Learner (L), Novice (N), then full licence\n- Quebec: learner licence, probationary licence, then regular licence\n\nEarly stages often include zero blood-alcohol tolerance and limits on passengers or night driving. Exact rules and waiting times vary by province.", lang: "en", topic: "transport" },
  { q: ["Can I exchange my foreign driver's licence in Canada?","convert my licence from another country","do i need to take a driving test as a newcomer"], a: "It depends on your province and the country that issued your licence. Many provinces have exchange agreements with certain countries, letting you swap your licence without road tests. Otherwise, you may need to take knowledge and road tests, and you may get credit for previous driving experience if you can prove it.\n\nYou can usually drive on a valid foreign licence for a limited time after arriving. Check your provincial licensing office for the current rules.", lang: "en", topic: "transport" },
  { q: ["I'm moving to another province. Do I need a new licence?","change drivers licence moving provinces","moving from ontario to bc licence"], a: "Yes. When you become a resident of a new province or territory, you must exchange your licence within a set period, often around 60 to 90 days, depending on the province. Exchanges between Canadian provinces are usually straightforward, often without tests if your licence is valid.\n\nYou'll also need to register and insure your vehicle in the new province, and you may need a vehicle inspection. Check your new province's licensing office.", lang: "en", topic: "transport" },
  { q: ["Any tips for winter driving in Canada?","how to drive in snow","first winter driving advice"], a: "Key winter driving tips:\n- Use winter tires; they grip far better below about 7°C.\n- Slow down and leave much more following distance.\n- Accelerate, brake and steer gently to avoid skids.\n- Clear all snow and ice from windows, lights and the roof before driving.\n- Watch for black ice, especially on bridges and shaded areas.\n- Keep your gas tank at least half full.\n- Check road conditions before long trips through your provincial 511 service.\n- Carry a winter emergency kit.", lang: "en", topic: "transport" },
  { q: ["Are winter tires mandatory in Canada?","do i need snow tires in ontario","winter tire law quebec bc"], a: "It depends on the province:\n- Quebec: winter tires are mandatory on passenger vehicles registered in Quebec during the winter period (currently December 1 to March 15).\n- British Columbia: winter tires or chains are required on many highways, generally starting October 1, with some routes extending into spring.\n- Elsewhere: not generally required, but strongly recommended.\n\nIn Ontario, insurers must offer a discount for winter tires. Look for the mountain-snowflake symbol, which marks true winter tires.", lang: "en", topic: "transport" },
  { q: ["What's the difference between winter tires and all-season tires?","are all season tires good enough for winter","m+s vs snowflake tires"], a: "Winter tires use rubber that stays soft in the cold, plus deeper treads and tiny grooves (sipes) that grip snow and ice. All-season tires harden below about 7°C and lose grip, so they can take much longer to stop on cold or icy roads.\n\nThe mountain-snowflake symbol marks tires that meet a severe snow standard. \"M+S\" (mud and snow) alone doesn't meet that standard and may not count where winter tires are required, as in Quebec.", lang: "en", topic: "transport" },
  { q: ["What should be in a winter car emergency kit?","winter survival kit for car","what to keep in my car in winter"], a: "A good winter car kit includes:\n- Ice scraper, snow brush and small shovel\n- Sand, kitty litter or traction mats\n- Booster cables\n- Warm blanket, extra hats, mittens and socks\n- Flashlight with extra batteries, or a hand-crank one\n- Phone charger or power bank\n- Water and non-perishable snacks\n- First-aid kit\n- Flares or reflective triangles\n- Windshield washer fluid rated for winter\n\nIf you're stuck, stay in your car and keep the exhaust pipe clear of snow.", lang: "en", topic: "transport" },
  { q: ["What should I do if my car skids on ice?","how to recover from a skid","car sliding on black ice what to do"], a: "Stay calm and avoid sudden moves:\n1. Take your foot off the gas.\n2. Look and steer gently where you want to go.\n3. Don't slam the brakes. With anti-lock brakes (ABS), press firmly and steadily and let the system pulse. Without ABS, pump the brakes gently.\n4. Avoid overcorrecting once the car straightens.\n\nPrevention is best: slow down, leave extra space and use winter tires. An empty parking lot is a safe place to practise.", lang: "en", topic: "transport" },
  { q: ["What is a block heater and do I need one?","should i plug in my car in winter","block heater explained"], a: "A block heater warms your engine's coolant or oil using electricity, so the engine starts more easily, warms up faster and wears less in extreme cold.\n\nIt's common in places with very cold winters, like the Prairies, the North and parts of Quebec and Ontario. Many people plug in when it's around −15°C or colder, for a few hours before driving; a timer saves electricity. Electric vehicles don't need one but benefit from preconditioning while plugged in.", lang: "en", topic: "transport" },
  { q: ["Do electric cars work in Canadian winters?","ev range in cold weather","is an electric car a good idea in canada"], a: "Yes, electric vehicles work in Canadian winters, but range drops in the cold. Batteries are less efficient at low temperatures, and heating the cabin uses energy.\n\nTips:\n- Precondition the cabin and battery while plugged in.\n- Use heated seats and steering wheel, which use less energy than heating the whole cabin.\n- Park in a garage if possible.\n- Plan charging stops on long trips.\n\nA heat pump helps efficiency. Use winter tires on EVs too.", lang: "en", topic: "transport" },
  { q: ["How do transit fare cards work in Canada?","presto compass opus cards","what card do i need for the bus in toronto"], a: "Many Canadian cities use reloadable fare cards:\n- Presto: Toronto (TTC), GO Transit and other Ontario systems including Ottawa's OC Transpo\n- Compass: Metro Vancouver (TransLink)\n- OPUS: Montreal (STM) and other Quebec systems\n\nYou tap the card when boarding, and some systems also need you to tap when exiting. Many now accept tapping a credit or debit card or phone directly. Fares and discounts for youth, seniors and others change, so check your transit agency's website.", lang: "en", topic: "transport" },
  { q: ["What is VIA Rail?","can i take the train across canada","train travel in canada"], a: "VIA Rail is Canada's national passenger rail service, a federal Crown corporation. Main services include:\n- The Corridor: frequent trains between Quebec City and Windsor, including Montreal, Ottawa and Toronto\n- The Canadian: a long-distance scenic route between Toronto and Vancouver\n- The Ocean: Montreal to Halifax\n- Several regional and remote routes\n\nSchedules and fares change, so check VIA Rail's website or ask me to look up current options.", lang: "en", topic: "transport" },
  { q: ["Tips for flying in Canada?","airport tips domestic flight canada","how early should i get to the airport"], a: "Helpful tips:\n- Arrive about 1.5 to 2 hours early for domestic flights, and around 3 hours for international ones.\n- Bring government-issued photo ID; you'll need a passport for international flights.\n- Carry-on liquids generally go in containers of 100 ml or less in one clear bag.\n- Check in online and confirm your baggage allowance, which varies by airline and fare.\n- Check flight status before leaving home, especially in winter.\n\nCanada has air passenger protection rules for delays and lost bags.", lang: "en", topic: "transport" },
  { q: ["My flight was delayed. Am I entitled to compensation?","flight cancelled compensation canada","air passenger protection regulations"], a: "Possibly. Canada's Air Passenger Protection Regulations set out what airlines owe for delays, cancellations, denied boarding and baggage problems. What you're entitled to depends on whether the cause was within the airline's control, and on how long the delay is.\n\nSteps:\n1. Ask the airline for the reason in writing.\n2. Keep receipts for meals or hotels.\n3. Submit a claim to the airline first.\n4. If unresolved, complain to the Canadian Transportation Agency.\n\nAmounts and rules can change.", lang: "en", topic: "transport" },
  { q: ["Is car insurance mandatory in Canada?","who provides car insurance in canada","public vs private auto insurance"], a: "Yes, car insurance is mandatory everywhere in Canada, and it's regulated provincially.\n\n- Public insurers provide basic coverage in British Columbia (ICBC), Saskatchewan (SGI) and Manitoba (MPI).\n- In Quebec, the SAAQ covers bodily injury, while private insurers cover property damage.\n- In other provinces, insurance is sold by private companies.\n\nRates depend on many factors and change, so get quotes from insurers or a licensed broker.", lang: "en", topic: "transport" },
  { q: ["How can I lower my car insurance?","car insurance too expensive tips","ways to save on auto insurance"], a: "I can't quote rates, but common ways to reduce premiums include:\n- Comparing quotes from several insurers, or using a licensed broker\n- Keeping a clean driving record\n- Choosing a higher deductible, if you can afford it\n- Bundling home and auto insurance\n- Asking about discounts, such as for winter tires (required in Ontario), telematics apps, or a security system\n- Considering the vehicle's insurance cost before buying\n\nIn provinces with public insurance, options differ.", lang: "en", topic: "transport" },
  { q: ["What should I do after a car accident?","car accident steps canada","i got in a fender bender what now"], a: "Steps after a collision:\n1. If anyone is hurt or there's danger, call 911.\n2. Move to safety if possible and turn on hazard lights.\n3. Exchange names, contact info, licence, plate and insurance details.\n4. Take photos of the vehicles, scene and damage.\n5. Get witness contact info.\n6. Report to police if required; many provinces require it above a certain damage amount or have collision reporting centres.\n7. Notify your insurer promptly.\n\nDon't admit fault at the scene.", lang: "en", topic: "transport" },
  { q: ["What should I check before buying a used car?","used car buying tips canada","how to avoid buying a lemon"], a: "Before you buy:\n- Get a vehicle history report to check for accidents and odometer issues.\n- Check for liens, so you don't inherit someone else's debt; some provinces offer lien searches.\n- Have an independent mechanic inspect it.\n- Test drive on city streets and the highway.\n- Check for rust, which is common in salty winter regions.\n- Confirm the seller's ID matches the ownership.\n\nYour province may require a safety inspection before registration.", lang: "en", topic: "transport" },
  { q: ["Can I turn right on a red light in Canada?","right on red montreal","is right turn on red legal in quebec"], a: "In most of Canada, yes: you can turn right on a red light after coming to a complete stop and yielding to pedestrians and traffic, unless a sign prohibits it.\n\nThe main exception is the island of Montreal, where right turns on red are not allowed. Elsewhere in Quebec it's generally permitted unless signs say otherwise. Always watch for signs at each intersection.", lang: "en", topic: "transport" },
  { q: ["What are the rules for school buses in Canada?","do i have to stop for a school bus","school bus flashing red lights"], a: "When a school bus has its red lights flashing and stop arm out, you must stop. In most cases this applies to traffic in both directions, unless the road is divided by a median, where generally only traffic behind the bus must stop.\n\nStay stopped until the lights stop flashing and the bus moves. Penalties are serious. Exact rules on stopping distances vary by province, so check your provincial driver's handbook.", lang: "en", topic: "transport" },
  { q: ["Is using my phone while driving illegal?","distracted driving laws canada","can i use gps on my phone while driving"], a: "Yes. Every province and territory bans using hand-held phones and devices while driving, including at red lights. Penalties can include fines, demerit points and licence suspension, especially for new drivers.\n\nHands-free use, like a mounted phone for GPS or voice calls, is generally allowed, but rules differ. Some provinces restrict touching the screen at all. The safest approach is to set your route before you start driving.", lang: "en", topic: "transport" },
  { q: ["What are the rules about impaired driving in Canada?","can i drive after one drink","is driving high illegal canada"], a: "Impaired driving by alcohol, cannabis or other drugs is a criminal offence under federal law, and provinces add their own penalties like immediate roadside suspensions.\n\nPolice can demand a breath sample from any lawfully stopped driver. Many provinces have zero-tolerance rules for new or young drivers. There's no reliable \"safe amount\" because everyone is affected differently. The safest choice: don't drive after drinking or using cannabis. Plan a ride, use transit or take a taxi.", lang: "en", topic: "transport" },
  { q: ["How do I get from the airport to downtown?","transit from airport canada","best way to get downtown from the airport"], a: "Options at most major Canadian airports include:\n- Public transit: such as the UP Express in Toronto, the Canada Line SkyTrain in Vancouver, and the 747 bus in Montreal\n- Taxis and rideshare apps from designated pickup areas\n- Airport shuttles to hotels\n- Car rentals\n\nTransit is usually cheapest; taxis are more convenient with luggage. Fares and schedules change, so tell me your airport and I can look up current options.", lang: "en", topic: "transport" },
  { q: ["How should I register my car in a new province?","vehicle registration canada","how to get licence plates"], a: "Vehicle registration is provincial. The general steps:\n1. Get car insurance valid in your province.\n2. Bring proof of ownership, your driver's licence and ID to your provincial registry office.\n3. Complete any required safety or emissions inspection; requirements vary.\n4. Pay registration fees and any applicable taxes.\n5. Receive your plates and registration.\n\nIf you're moving from another province, there's usually a deadline to re-register. Check your provincial licensing office.", lang: "en", topic: "transport" },
  { q: ["How does health care work in Canada?","is healthcare free in canada","canadian health system explained"], a: "Canada has publicly funded health care, often called medicare. Each province and territory runs its own health insurance plan under national principles set by the Canada Health Act.\n\nMedically necessary doctor and hospital services are covered for eligible residents, with no charge at the point of care. Many things are not fully covered, such as most prescription drugs outside hospital, dental care, vision care and physiotherapy, though coverage varies by province and some public programs help. Many people have extra coverage through work.", lang: "en", topic: "health" },
  { q: ["What are OHIP, RAMQ, MSP and AHCIP?","provincial health card names","what is my health insurance called in my province"], a: "These are provincial health insurance plans:\n- OHIP: Ontario Health Insurance Plan\n- RAMQ: Régie de l'assurance maladie du Québec, which runs Quebec's plan\n- MSP: Medical Services Plan of British Columbia\n- AHCIP: Alberta Health Care Insurance Plan\n\nEvery province and territory has its own plan. Once enrolled, you get a health card, which you show when you see a doctor or go to the hospital. Keep it safe and renew it when required.", lang: "en", topic: "health" },
  { q: ["How do I get a health card in Canada?","apply for ohip as a newcomer","is there a waiting period for health coverage"], a: "Apply through your provincial or territorial health insurance plan, usually at a service office or online. You'll typically need:\n- Proof of identity\n- Proof of residency in the province\n- Proof of immigration or citizenship status\n\nSome provinces have a waiting period for new residents, and eligibility rules change. If you're new, consider private health insurance to cover any gap. Check your province's health ministry for current rules.", lang: "en", topic: "health" },
  { q: ["How do I find a family doctor?","no family doctor what do i do","how to get a gp in canada"], a: "Ways to find a family doctor or nurse practitioner:\n- Register with your province's patient waitlist or attachment program, such as Health Care Connect in Ontario, the Guichet d'accès à un médecin de famille (GAMF) in Quebec, or the Health Connect Registry in BC.\n- Ask local clinics, community health centres and friends whether anyone is accepting patients.\n- Check your provincial college of physicians' directory.\n\nWait times can be long, so use walk-in clinics, virtual care or 811 meanwhile.", lang: "en", topic: "health" },
  { q: ["What is a walk-in clinic?","where can i see a doctor without an appointment","walk in clinic vs emergency room"], a: "A walk-in clinic lets you see a doctor or nurse practitioner without being a regular patient, often the same day. It's good for non-urgent issues like minor infections, rashes, prescription renewals and minor injuries.\n\nBring your health card. Visits are usually covered by your provincial plan for eligible residents. Some clinics allow online booking or check-in to reduce waiting.\n\nFor severe symptoms like chest pain, trouble breathing or signs of stroke, call 911 instead.", lang: "en", topic: "health" },
  { q: ["When should I go to the emergency room?","er or walk in clinic","is this serious enough for emergency"], a: "Call 911 or go to the emergency department for serious or life-threatening problems, such as:\n- Chest pain or pressure\n- Trouble breathing\n- Signs of stroke: face drooping, arm weakness, slurred speech\n- Severe bleeding, major injuries or burns\n- Sudden confusion or loss of consciousness\n- Severe allergic reactions\n- Thoughts of harming yourself or others\n\nFor less urgent concerns, try a walk-in clinic, urgent care, your doctor, a pharmacist or your provincial 811 line.", lang: "en", topic: "health" },
  { q: ["What is 811?","nurse hotline canada","is there a number to call a nurse for free"], a: "In most provinces and territories, dialling 811 connects you to a free, confidential health line staffed by registered nurses. They can assess your symptoms, advise whether you need care and where, and answer general health questions.\n\nIt's often available 24/7, with service in multiple languages or interpretation. Names differ, such as Health Link in Alberta, HealthLink BC and Info-Santé in Quebec. For emergencies, always call 911 first.", lang: "en", topic: "health" },
  { q: ["Can a pharmacist prescribe medication in Canada?","pharmacist prescribe for uti","can i see a pharmacist instead of a doctor"], a: "In many provinces, yes. Pharmacists can assess and prescribe for certain minor conditions, such as uncomplicated urinary tract infections, cold sores, pink eye, allergies, and some skin conditions. They can often renew existing prescriptions too.\n\nWhich conditions are covered, and whether there's a fee, varies by province and changes over time. Call your local pharmacy to ask what they can help with. Pharmacists can also tell you when you need to see a doctor.", lang: "en", topic: "health" },
  { q: ["What is virtual care?","can i see a doctor online in canada","telehealth canada"], a: "Virtual care means seeing a health professional by phone, video or secure messaging instead of in person. It's useful for prescription renewals, follow-ups, mental health appointments and many minor concerns.\n\nOptions include your own doctor's virtual visits, provincial virtual care services, and private apps. Whether a virtual visit is covered by your provincial plan depends on the province and service, so check before booking. Some problems still need an in-person exam.", lang: "en", topic: "health" },
  { q: ["Does provincial health insurance cover prescriptions?","who pays for medication in canada","are prescriptions free in canada"], a: "Generally, prescription drugs you take at home aren't fully covered by provincial health insurance for everyone, but coverage varies:\n- Provincial drug programs often help seniors, people on social assistance, youth in some provinces, and people with high drug costs.\n- Quebec requires residents to have prescription drug coverage, either through a private group plan or the public RAMQ plan.\n- Many people have coverage through workplace benefits.\n\nA national pharmacare framework has started covering some drugs; details are evolving.", lang: "en", topic: "health" },
  { q: ["Is dental care covered in Canada?","canadian dental care plan","how do i get help paying for the dentist"], a: "Dental care isn't covered by provincial health insurance for most adults. Options include:\n- The Canadian Dental Care Plan (CDCP): a federal program that helps eligible residents without dental insurance and with lower family incomes. Eligibility has been rolled out in phases.\n- Provincial programs for children, seniors or people on social assistance.\n- Workplace benefits.\n- Dental school clinics, which may offer lower-cost care.\n\nEligibility and coverage change, so check canada.ca for current CDCP details.", lang: "en", topic: "health" },
  { q: ["Is eye care covered by health insurance?","do i have to pay for an eye exam","glasses covered ohip?"], a: "It depends on your age, province and health conditions. Many provinces cover routine eye exams for children and seniors, and exams related to certain medical conditions like diabetes. Most adults pay for routine exams themselves.\n\nGlasses and contact lenses are generally not covered by provincial plans, though some programs help children or low-income residents, and many workplace benefits plans cover part of the cost. Check your provincial plan for specifics.", lang: "en", topic: "health" },
  { q: ["I'm in crisis and need someone to talk to","suicide hotline canada","i don't want to be alive anymore"], a: "I'm really glad you reached out. You can call or text 9-8-8, the Suicide Crisis Helpline, anytime, 24/7, for free, confidential support from a trained responder in English or French.\n\nIf you're in immediate danger or might act on these thoughts, call 911 or go to the nearest emergency department now.\n\nIf you'd like, you can tell me what's going on. You don't have to go through this alone.", lang: "en", topic: "health" },
  { q: ["Where can I get mental health support in Canada?","free counselling canada","i feel anxious all the time who can help"], a: "There are several options:\n- Your family doctor or a walk-in clinic can assess you and refer you.\n- 811 in many provinces can connect you to services.\n- Community health centres and local Canadian Mental Health Association branches offer programs.\n- Employee assistance programs (EAPs) often include free short-term counselling.\n- Campus counselling for students.\n- Kids Help Phone for young people.\n\nIn a crisis, call or text 988 anytime. If you're in immediate danger, call 911.", lang: "en", topic: "health" },
  { q: ["Are psychologists covered by OHIP or other provincial plans?","does health insurance pay for therapy","how to afford therapy in canada"], a: "Generally, private psychologists, psychotherapists and counsellors are not covered by provincial health plans. Services from psychiatrists (medical doctors) and those offered in hospitals or some public programs are usually covered.\n\nWays to reduce costs:\n- Workplace benefits or employee assistance programs\n- Publicly funded programs; some provinces offer free short-term therapy\n- Sliding-scale fees at community agencies\n- Student counselling services\n\nYour doctor can help you find options.", lang: "en", topic: "health" },
  { q: ["How can I build healthier habits?","tips to be healthier","how to start eating better and exercising"], a: "Start small and be consistent:\n- Movement: Canadian guidelines suggest adults aim for about 150 minutes of moderate-to-vigorous activity a week, plus strength activities. Walking counts.\n- Food: Canada's Food Guide recommends filling half your plate with vegetables and fruits, choosing whole grains and protein foods, and drinking water.\n- Sleep: most adults need 7 to 9 hours.\n- Habit stacking: attach a new habit to an existing one.\n\nTalk to your doctor before major changes if you have health conditions.", lang: "en", topic: "health" },
  { q: ["What does Canada's Food Guide recommend?","canada food guide plate","healthy eating canada"], a: "Canada's Food Guide focuses on eating patterns:\n- Fill half your plate with vegetables and fruits.\n- A quarter with protein foods, choosing plant-based proteins more often.\n- A quarter with whole grain foods.\n- Make water your drink of choice.\n\nIt also encourages cooking more often, eating with others, reading food labels and limiting highly processed foods. For specific dietary needs, a registered dietitian can help.", lang: "en", topic: "health" },
  { q: ["How can I sleep better?","can't sleep tips","sleep hygiene"], a: "Try these sleep habits:\n- Keep a consistent sleep and wake time, even on weekends.\n- Get daylight in the morning; this matters in dark Canadian winters.\n- Limit caffeine after early afternoon and avoid heavy meals or alcohol close to bedtime.\n- Keep your room dark, quiet and cool.\n- Put screens away 30 to 60 minutes before bed.\n- If you can't sleep after about 20 minutes, get up and do something calm until sleepy.\n\nIf problems last weeks, talk to your doctor.", lang: "en", topic: "health" },
  { q: ["What is seasonal affective disorder?","winter depression","i feel down every winter is that normal"], a: "Seasonal affective disorder (SAD) is a type of depression that follows a seasonal pattern, usually starting in fall or winter when there's less daylight. Symptoms can include low mood, low energy, oversleeping, craving carbohydrates and withdrawing from others.\n\nHelpful steps include getting outside in daylight, staying active and keeping social contact. Light therapy and other treatments can help, but talk to a doctor first to get the right diagnosis and advice. If you're in crisis, call or text 988.", lang: "en", topic: "health" },
  { q: ["Do I need vitamin D in Canada?","vitamin d winter canada","should i take vitamin d"], a: "Vitamin D helps your body absorb calcium for healthy bones. Your skin makes it from sunlight, but in much of Canada the winter sun is too weak for much vitamin D production from roughly October to March.\n\nIt's also found in fatty fish, egg yolks, and fortified milk and plant-based beverages. Whether you need a supplement, and how much, depends on your age, diet and health. Ask your doctor or pharmacist.", lang: "en", topic: "health" },
  { q: ["Where can I get vaccinated in Canada?","flu shot where","how do i get my vaccines as an adult"], a: "Common places to get vaccines:\n- Your family doctor or nurse practitioner\n- Pharmacies, which offer many vaccines including the seasonal flu shot in most provinces\n- Public health units or CLSCs in Quebec\n- Some walk-in clinics\n\nRoutine childhood vaccines are publicly funded. Which adult vaccines are free depends on your province, age and health. Keep a record of your vaccinations, and ask a pharmacist or public health which ones you may need.", lang: "en", topic: "health" },
  { q: ["Why do kids need vaccine records for school?","school immunization requirements","my child got a letter about vaccines from school"], a: "Some provinces, such as Ontario and New Brunswick, require students to have certain vaccines or a valid exemption to attend school. Public health uses school records to protect students during outbreaks.\n\nIf you get a letter, it usually means the records on file are incomplete. Contact your local public health unit or your doctor to update records or catch up on missing vaccines. Many routine childhood vaccines are free.", lang: "en", topic: "health" },
  { q: ["I have a bad cough and fever. What should I do?","sore throat and fever what should i take","feeling sick what do i do"], a: "I can't diagnose you, but here's how to get help:\n- Call 811 (in most provinces) to speak with a nurse about your symptoms.\n- Ask a pharmacist about symptom relief and whether you need to see a doctor.\n- See your doctor, a walk-in clinic or virtual care if symptoms are severe, getting worse, or lasting more than a few days.\n\nCall 911 right away for trouble breathing, chest pain, confusion or bluish lips. Meanwhile, rest, drink fluids and stay home to avoid spreading illness.", lang: "en", topic: "health" },
  { q: ["What are the signs of a stroke?","stroke symptoms FAST","how do i know if someone is having a stroke"], a: "Use FAST:\n- Face: is it drooping?\n- Arms: can they raise both arms?\n- Speech: is it slurred or jumbled?\n- Time: call 911 right away.\n\nOther signs can include sudden numbness, vision problems, severe headache or loss of balance. Stroke is a medical emergency; fast treatment can reduce brain damage. Note the time symptoms started, and don't drive the person to the hospital yourself. Call 911.", lang: "en", topic: "health" },
  { q: ["What are the signs of a heart attack?","heart attack symptoms","chest pain what to do"], a: "Call 911 immediately if someone has possible heart attack signs:\n- Chest pain, pressure, squeezing or discomfort\n- Pain spreading to the arms, neck, jaw, back or shoulders\n- Shortness of breath\n- Sweating, nausea, light-headedness\n\nWomen, older adults and people with diabetes may have subtler symptoms like fatigue, nausea or back pain. Stop all activity and sit or lie down. A 911 dispatcher may tell you whether to chew aspirin. Don't drive yourself.", lang: "en", topic: "health" },
  { q: ["How do I prevent frostbite?","frostbite signs","what to do if fingers are frozen"], a: "To prevent frostbite: cover exposed skin, wear layers, mittens rather than gloves, a hat and a scarf, stay dry, and limit time outside when wind chill is very low.\n\nEarly signs (frostnip): numbness and red or pale skin. Frostbite: skin turns white or greyish-yellow, waxy or hard.\n\nWhat to do: get indoors and rewarm gently in warm (not hot) water. Don't rub the skin or use direct heat. Seek medical care for anything beyond mild frostnip.", lang: "en", topic: "health" },
  { q: ["How much exercise do adults need?","how many minutes of exercise per week","canadian physical activity guidelines"], a: "Canada's 24-Hour Movement Guidelines for adults aged 18 to 64 recommend:\n- At least 150 minutes per week of moderate-to-vigorous aerobic activity, such as brisk walking, cycling or swimming\n- Muscle-strengthening activities at least twice a week\n- Several hours of light activity, including standing\n- Limiting sedentary time and getting 7 to 9 hours of sleep\n\nAny amount is better than none. Check with your doctor before starting if you have health concerns.", lang: "en", topic: "health" },
  { q: ["What happens when I call 911?","when to call 911 canada","is 911 free"], a: "911 is free from any phone, including cell phones without a plan. Call it for emergencies needing police, fire or ambulance immediately.\n\nWhen you call:\n- Say which service you need.\n- Give your exact location first.\n- Describe what's happening.\n- Stay on the line and follow instructions.\n\nDon't hang up if you dialled by mistake; stay on and explain. For non-emergencies, use your local police non-emergency number or 811 for health advice.", lang: "en", topic: "health" },
  { q: ["How do I reduce stress?","stress management tips","i'm so stressed at work"], a: "Practical ways to manage stress:\n- Move your body; even a 10-minute walk helps.\n- Breathe slowly: in for 4 seconds, out for 6, for a few minutes.\n- Sleep and eat regularly.\n- Break big problems into small next steps.\n- Talk to someone you trust.\n- Set boundaries on work and screen time.\n- Limit alcohol and caffeine.\n\nIf stress is affecting daily life, talk to your doctor or check if your workplace has an employee assistance program. In a crisis, call or text 988.", lang: "en", topic: "health" },
  { q: ["What should be in a home first-aid kit?","first aid kit checklist","basic first aid supplies"], a: "A good home first-aid kit includes:\n- Adhesive bandages in various sizes\n- Sterile gauze pads and medical tape\n- Elastic bandage\n- Antiseptic wipes\n- Tweezers and scissors\n- Disposable gloves\n- Instant cold pack\n- Digital thermometer\n- A first-aid manual\n- Emergency phone numbers\n\nCheck expiry dates yearly. A first-aid course, such as those from the Canadian Red Cross or St. John Ambulance, teaches you how to use it.", lang: "en", topic: "health" },
  { q: ["What is artificial intelligence?","explain ai simply","what does ai actually mean"], a: "Artificial intelligence (AI) is technology that lets computers do tasks that normally need human intelligence, like understanding language, recognizing images, making predictions or solving problems.\n\nMost modern AI uses machine learning: instead of being programmed with every rule, a system learns patterns from large amounts of example data. AI is used in voice assistants, spam filters, maps, translation and recommendations. It can be very useful but can also make mistakes, so important information is worth double-checking.", lang: "en", topic: "technology" },
  { q: ["How do AI chatbots like you work?","how does a large language model work","are you actually thinking"], a: "AI assistants like me are built on large language models. They're trained on huge amounts of text to learn patterns in language, then refined to be helpful and safe. When you ask something, the model generates a response by predicting likely next words based on everything it learned and the conversation so far.\n\nThis lets me explain, summarize and draft text, but I can make mistakes, so verify important facts. For current information, I can look things up live.", lang: "en", topic: "technology" },
  { q: ["What is machine learning?","machine learning vs ai","how do computers learn"], a: "Machine learning is a branch of AI where computers learn from data instead of following only hand-written rules.\n\nExample: to filter spam, you show a system thousands of emails labelled \"spam\" or \"not spam.\" It learns which patterns tend to signal spam and then applies them to new emails.\n\nMain types:\n- Supervised learning: learns from labelled examples\n- Unsupervised learning: finds patterns in unlabelled data\n- Reinforcement learning: learns by trial and error with rewards", lang: "en", topic: "technology" },
  { q: ["What is the cloud?","what does cloud storage mean","explain cloud computing simply"], a: "\"The cloud\" means using computers, storage and software owned by a company and accessed over the internet, instead of on your own device.\n\nExamples:\n- Storing photos online so they sync across your phone and laptop\n- Using email or documents in a web browser\n- Streaming music and video\n\nBusinesses rent cloud servers instead of buying their own. The data is still on physical computers in data centres, just not yours. Strong passwords and two-factor authentication help keep cloud accounts safe.", lang: "en", topic: "technology" },
  { q: ["How do I create a strong password?","password tips","how to make a good password i can remember"], a: "A strong password is long and unique:\n- Use at least 12 to 15 characters; length matters most.\n- Try a passphrase of 4 or more random words, like \"maple-canoe-orbit-pickle\" (don't use this exact one).\n- Never reuse passwords across accounts.\n- Avoid personal info like names, birthdays or pet names.\n- Use a password manager to create and store them.\n\nTurn on two-factor authentication for important accounts like email and banking.", lang: "en", topic: "technology" },
  { q: ["What is two-factor authentication?","2fa explained","should i turn on two step verification"], a: "Two-factor authentication (2FA) adds a second check when you log in, besides your password. Even if someone steals your password, they can't get in without the second factor.\n\nCommon second factors, from strongest to weakest:\n- Security keys or passkeys\n- Authenticator apps that generate codes\n- Text-message (SMS) codes, which are better than nothing\n\nYes, turn it on, especially for email, banking, social media and your phone account. Save your backup codes somewhere safe.", lang: "en", topic: "technology" },
  { q: ["What is a password manager?","are password managers safe","should i use a password manager"], a: "A password manager is an app that creates, stores and fills in strong, unique passwords for all your accounts. You only need to remember one strong master password.\n\nReputable password managers encrypt your data so even the company can't read it. They're generally much safer than reusing passwords or writing them in a notes file. Many browsers and phones have one built in. Protect your master password and turn on two-factor authentication for the manager itself.", lang: "en", topic: "technology" },
  { q: ["What is phishing?","how to spot a phishing email","is this email a scam"], a: "Phishing is when scammers pretend to be a trusted organization to trick you into clicking a link, sharing passwords or sending money.\n\nWarning signs:\n- Urgency or threats: \"Your account will be closed!\"\n- Sender addresses that don't match the real organization\n- Links that go to odd web addresses; hover to check\n- Requests for passwords, codes or payment\n- Unexpected attachments\n\nWhen unsure, don't click. Go to the official website or app directly, or call a number you trust.", lang: "en", topic: "technology" },
  { q: ["I got a text saying I owe the CRA money. Is it real?","cra scam call","someone called saying they're from canada revenue agency and i'll be arrested"], a: "It's very likely a scam. The CRA won't:\n- Threaten arrest or deportation\n- Demand payment by gift cards, prepaid credit cards or cryptocurrency\n- Ask for personal info by text or instant messaging\n- Send links in texts asking for payment\n\nDon't reply or click. To check whether you really owe anything, sign in to CRA My Account yourself or call the CRA using the number on canada.ca. Report scams to the Canadian Anti-Fraud Centre.", lang: "en", topic: "technology" },
  { q: ["What are common scams in Canada?","how to avoid scams","scams targeting seniors canada"], a: "Common scams include:\n- Impersonation: fake CRA, police, bank or Canada Post messages\n- Grandparent scams: a \"grandchild\" urgently needs bail money\n- Romance scams that eventually ask for money\n- Fake online shops and marketplace deals\n- Investment and crypto scams promising big returns\n- Tech support pop-ups asking for remote access\n- Job scams\n\nThe golden rules: slow down, verify independently, and never pay with gift cards or crypto under pressure. Report scams to the Canadian Anti-Fraud Centre.", lang: "en", topic: "technology" },
  { q: ["I think I got scammed. What should I do?","i sent money to a scammer","my account was hacked what now"], a: "Act quickly:\n1. Contact your bank or credit card company right away to stop or dispute payments.\n2. Change passwords on affected accounts, starting with email, and turn on two-factor authentication.\n3. Report it to local police and the Canadian Anti-Fraud Centre.\n4. If your identity info was shared, contact Equifax and TransUnion Canada about fraud alerts.\n5. Watch for follow-up \"recovery\" scams promising to get your money back.\n\nIt's not your fault; scammers are professionals.", lang: "en", topic: "technology" },
  { q: ["How does Wi-Fi work?","what is wifi","difference between wifi and internet"], a: "Wi-Fi is a way of connecting devices to a network wirelessly using radio waves. Your router creates a local network in your home, and it's connected to your internet service provider through a modem.\n\nSo Wi-Fi isn't the internet itself; it's the wireless link between your devices and your router. If Wi-Fi works but nothing loads, the problem may be your internet connection. Restarting the modem and router often fixes it.", lang: "en", topic: "technology" },
  { q: ["How can I improve my Wi-Fi signal?","wifi is slow at home","how to fix weak wifi"], a: "Try these fixes:\n- Restart your modem and router.\n- Place the router centrally and up high, away from thick walls, metal and microwaves.\n- Use a 5 GHz band for speed nearby, and 2.4 GHz for longer range.\n- Update the router's firmware.\n- Use a mesh system or extender for large homes.\n- Plug in devices like TVs or computers with an Ethernet cable.\n- Check if your internet plan is fast enough for your household.\n\nIf problems continue, contact your provider.", lang: "en", topic: "technology" },
  { q: ["Is public Wi-Fi safe?","using coffee shop wifi","should i use a vpn on public wifi"], a: "Public Wi-Fi is convenient but riskier. Others on the same network may try to snoop, and fake hotspots with familiar names exist.\n\nTips:\n- Confirm the exact network name with staff.\n- Stick to sites using HTTPS; most major sites do.\n- Avoid banking or sensitive tasks if you can; use mobile data instead.\n- Turn off auto-connect to open networks.\n- A reputable VPN adds encryption.\n- Keep your device updated.", lang: "en", topic: "technology" },
  { q: ["How does the internet work?","explain the internet simply","what happens when i type a website address"], a: "The internet is a global network of connected computers that exchange data using shared rules called protocols.\n\nWhen you visit a website:\n1. Your device asks a DNS server to translate the name (like canada.ca) into an IP address, a numeric address.\n2. Your request travels through your router and internet provider across many networks.\n3. The website's server sends back the page in small packets.\n4. Your browser reassembles them and displays the page.\n\nThis usually happens in a fraction of a second.", lang: "en", topic: "technology" },
  { q: ["What does HTTPS and the padlock mean?","is the padlock icon safe","http vs https"], a: "HTTPS means the connection between your browser and the website is encrypted, so others on the network can't easily read or change what's sent, like passwords or card numbers. The padlock icon shows this.\n\nImportant: the padlock does not mean the site is trustworthy. Scam sites can use HTTPS too. Always check the web address carefully, especially for banking and shopping.", lang: "en", topic: "technology" },
  { q: ["Why should I update my phone and apps?","are software updates important","should i install updates right away"], a: "Updates often fix security holes that attackers can exploit, as well as bugs, and they sometimes add features. Outdated software is one of the most common ways devices get compromised.\n\nTips:\n- Turn on automatic updates for your phone, computer, browser and apps.\n- Restart when prompted; many updates finish on restart.\n- Replace devices that no longer receive security updates.\n- Download apps only from official app stores.", lang: "en", topic: "technology" },
  { q: ["How can I protect my privacy online?","online privacy tips","how to stop apps tracking me"], a: "Practical privacy steps:\n- Review app permissions and turn off location, microphone or contacts access when not needed.\n- Limit what you share publicly on social media.\n- Check privacy settings on your accounts.\n- Use a browser with tracking protection.\n- Be careful with quizzes and forms that ask for personal info.\n- Delete apps you no longer use.\n- Use different emails for important accounts and sign-ups.\n\nIn Canada, federal and provincial privacy laws give you rights over how businesses collect and use your personal information.", lang: "en", topic: "technology" },
  { q: ["How do I keep my kids safe online?","parental controls tips","my child wants a phone how to keep them safe"], a: "Combine tools with conversation:\n- Use built-in parental controls on devices, apps and your router.\n- Keep devices in shared spaces for younger kids.\n- Check age ratings and privacy settings on games and social apps.\n- Talk openly about strangers, scams, sharing photos and cyberbullying.\n- Agree on screen-time rules together.\n- Make sure they know they can come to you without getting in trouble.\n\nCanada's Cybertip.ca accepts reports of online sexual exploitation of children.", lang: "en", topic: "technology" },
  { q: ["How do I back up my phone and computer?","backup tips","what's the best way to back up photos"], a: "A common guideline is the 3-2-1 rule: keep 3 copies of important data, on 2 different types of storage, with 1 copy off-site, such as in the cloud.\n\nOptions:\n- Turn on your phone's built-in cloud backup.\n- Use an external drive with your computer's backup tool.\n- Use a cloud storage service for photos and documents.\n\nTest a restore occasionally to make sure your backups actually work.", lang: "en", topic: "technology" },
  { q: ["What is malware?","how do i know if my computer has a virus","ransomware explained"], a: "Malware is malicious software designed to harm devices, steal data or take control. Types include viruses, spyware, and ransomware, which locks files and demands payment.\n\nWarning signs: sudden slowness, pop-ups, unknown apps, settings changing on their own, or friends getting strange messages from you.\n\nProtection: keep software updated, use built-in security tools, avoid pirated software and suspicious attachments, and back up your files. If infected, disconnect from the internet and run a security scan.", lang: "en", topic: "technology" },
  { q: ["What is coding?","how do i start learning to code","best programming language for beginners"], a: "Coding is writing instructions that a computer can follow, using a programming language.\n\nTo start:\n- Python is a popular first language because it's readable and widely used for data, automation and AI.\n- JavaScript is the language of the web and runs in every browser.\n- Use free resources like online tutorials, library courses and coding communities.\n- Build small projects: a calculator, a to-do list, a simple website.\n\nPractise a little every day; consistency beats long, rare sessions.", lang: "en", topic: "technology" },
  { q: ["What is a variable in programming?","explain variables loops and if statements","basic programming concepts"], a: "Core programming ideas:\n- Variable: a named container for a value, like age = 30.\n- Conditional (if/else): runs code only when something is true, like \"if age >= 18, show the adult price.\"\n- Loop: repeats code, like \"for each item in the list, print it.\"\n- Function: a reusable block of code with a name, like calculate_tip(bill).\n- Data types: numbers, text (strings), true/false (booleans) and lists.\n\nAlmost every program combines these building blocks.", lang: "en", topic: "technology" },
  { q: ["What does open source mean?","open source software explained","is open source free"], a: "Open source software is software whose source code is publicly available for anyone to view, use, change and share, under a licence that sets the rules.\n\nExamples include Linux, Firefox, LibreOffice, Python and Android's core. Benefits include transparency, community collaboration, and often no cost. \"Open source\" is about freedom to use and modify, not necessarily price; some companies sell support or services around open source software.", lang: "en", topic: "technology" },
  { q: ["What's the difference between hardware and software?","hardware vs software","what is an operating system"], a: "Hardware is the physical parts of a device: screen, processor, memory, storage, keyboard.\n\nSoftware is the instructions that run on it: apps, programs and the operating system.\n\nThe operating system (OS), such as Windows, macOS, Linux, Android or iOS, manages the hardware and lets other apps run. Think of hardware as the body, the OS as the nervous system, and apps as specific skills.", lang: "en", topic: "technology" },
  { q: ["What's the difference between RAM and storage?","ram vs storage","how much memory do i need"], a: "RAM (memory) is short-term working space. It holds what you're using right now, like open apps and browser tabs. More RAM lets you run more at once without slowing down. It's cleared when you turn the device off.\n\nStorage (SSD or hard drive) holds your files, photos and apps long-term, even when powered off.\n\nAnalogy: RAM is your desk space; storage is your filing cabinet. How much you need depends on what you do.", lang: "en", topic: "technology" },
  { q: ["How can I make my phone battery last longer?","phone battery dies fast","battery saving tips"], a: "Tips to extend daily battery life:\n- Lower screen brightness or use auto-brightness and dark mode.\n- Turn on battery saver mode.\n- Check which apps use the most battery in settings and limit their background activity.\n- Turn off location, Bluetooth or Wi-Fi when not needed.\n- Keep software updated.\n\nFor long-term battery health, avoid extreme heat and cold; Canadian winters can drain batteries fast, so keep your phone in an inside pocket.", lang: "en", topic: "technology" },
  { q: ["What is a passkey?","passkeys vs passwords","should i use passkeys"], a: "A passkey is a newer way to sign in without a password. It uses a pair of cryptographic keys: one stays securely on your device, and the website only stores the matching public key. You unlock it with your fingerprint, face or device PIN.\n\nBenefits: nothing to remember, nothing for hackers to steal from the website, and strong protection against phishing, since passkeys only work on the real site. If an account offers passkeys, it's generally a good choice.", lang: "en", topic: "technology" },
  { q: ["What is a VPN?","do i need a vpn","what does a vpn do"], a: "A VPN (virtual private network) creates an encrypted tunnel between your device and a VPN server. It hides your traffic from others on the same network, like public Wi-Fi, and makes your connection appear to come from the VPN server's location.\n\nWhat it doesn't do: make you anonymous, stop phishing, or protect against malware. You're also trusting the VPN company with your traffic, so choose a reputable one. Many workplaces use VPNs for secure remote access.", lang: "en", topic: "technology" },
  { q: ["How do I spot misinformation online?","how to tell if news is fake","is this ai generated image real"], a: "Before believing or sharing:\n- Check the source: is it a known, credible outlet?\n- Look for the same story from other reliable sources.\n- Read beyond the headline.\n- Check the date; old stories get recirculated.\n- Be wary of content designed to make you angry or afraid.\n- Do a reverse image search for suspicious photos.\n- Watch for AI-generated images with odd hands, text or lighting.\n\nWhen in doubt, don't share.", lang: "en", topic: "technology" },
  { q: ["Where was Shopify founded?","tell me about shopify","is shopify canadian"], a: "Yes, Shopify is Canadian. It was founded in Ottawa in 2006 by Tobias Lütke and Scott Lake, and grew out of an online snowboard store called Snowdevil. Lütke built his own e-commerce software for it, which became Shopify.\n\nShopify provides a platform that lets businesses of all sizes create online stores, sell in person and manage payments, shipping and inventory. For current leadership, financials or news, I can look that up live.", lang: "en", topic: "companies" },
  { q: ["What are the big five banks in Canada?","biggest banks in canada","list canada's big 5 banks"], a: "Canada's \"Big Five\" banks are:\n- RBC (Royal Bank of Canada)\n- TD (Toronto-Dominion Bank)\n- Scotiabank (Bank of Nova Scotia)\n- BMO (Bank of Montreal)\n- CIBC (Canadian Imperial Bank of Commerce), formed in 1961 by merging the Canadian Bank of Commerce and the Imperial Bank of Canada\n\nThey offer personal and business banking, lending, wealth management and investment services, and several have major US operations. National Bank, based in Montreal, is often counted as the sixth.", lang: "en", topic: "companies" },
  { q: ["Tell me about RBC","where was royal bank founded","history of royal bank of canada"], a: "RBC (Royal Bank of Canada) began in 1864 in Halifax as the Merchants Bank of Halifax and took the name Royal Bank of Canada in 1901. It later moved its head office to Montreal, and today its main operations are based in Toronto.\n\nRBC provides personal and commercial banking, wealth management, insurance, investor services and capital markets. For current leadership, share price or results, I can look them up live.", lang: "en", topic: "companies" },
  { q: ["What is TD Bank's history?","what does td stand for","tell me about td"], a: "TD stands for Toronto-Dominion. The bank was formed in 1955 through the merger of The Bank of Toronto (founded 1855) and The Dominion Bank (founded 1869). It's headquartered in Toronto.\n\nTD offers personal and commercial banking, wealth management and investment services. It also has a large US retail banking presence under the TD Bank name. For current details or news, I can check live.", lang: "en", topic: "companies" },
  { q: ["Tell me about Scotiabank","is scotiabank from nova scotia","bank of nova scotia history"], a: "Scotiabank's legal name is The Bank of Nova Scotia. It was founded in 1832 in Halifax, Nova Scotia, and its executive offices are now in Toronto.\n\nScotiabank offers personal, commercial and corporate banking, wealth management and capital markets services. It has historically been one of the most international Canadian banks, with operations in several countries in the Americas. For current leadership or financials, I can look that up live.", lang: "en", topic: "companies" },
  { q: ["What is Canada's oldest bank?","tell me about BMO","when was bank of montreal founded"], a: "BMO (Bank of Montreal) is Canada's oldest bank, founded in 1817 in Montreal. It even served as Canada's government banker before the Bank of Canada was created in 1934, and issued some of the country's early banknotes.\n\nBMO's legal head office is in Montreal and its operational head office is in Toronto. It offers personal and commercial banking, wealth management and capital markets services, with a large US presence. For current news or numbers, I can check live.", lang: "en", topic: "companies" },
  { q: ["Who founded Tim Hortons?","where did tim hortons start","tell me about tim hortons"], a: "Tim Hortons was founded in 1964 in Hamilton, Ontario, by NHL hockey player Tim Horton. Businessman Ron Joyce soon became a partner and expanded the chain after Horton's death in 1974.\n\nKnown for coffee, doughnuts and Timbits, it became a Canadian cultural icon, with locations across the country and abroad. Since 2014, it has been part of Restaurant Brands International, formed with Burger King. For current details, I can look them up live.", lang: "en", topic: "companies" },
  { q: ["What's the history of Canadian Tire?","who started canadian tire","is canadian tire money still a thing"], a: "Canadian Tire was founded in 1922 in Toronto by brothers John W. Billes and Alfred J. Billes, who bought a garage and auto-parts business. It grew into a national retailer selling automotive, hardware, sports, leisure and home products.\n\nIts paper \"Canadian Tire money\" coupons date back to 1958 and are still recognized as a quirky Canadian tradition, alongside its digital loyalty program. The company also owns other retail brands. For current details, I can check live.", lang: "en", topic: "companies" },
  { q: ["Where is Lululemon from?","who founded lululemon","is lululemon canadian"], a: "Yes, Lululemon is Canadian. It was founded in 1998 in Vancouver, British Columbia, by Chip Wilson. Its first store opened in Vancouver's Kitsilano neighbourhood, and the company is still headquartered in Vancouver.\n\nLululemon designs and sells athletic apparel, especially yoga wear, along with clothing for running, training and everyday wear. For current leadership, sales or news, I can look that up live.", lang: "en", topic: "companies" },
  { q: ["What happened to BlackBerry?","where is blackberry from","tell me about research in motion"], a: "BlackBerry started as Research In Motion (RIM), founded in 1984 in Waterloo, Ontario, by Mike Lazaridis and Douglas Fregin. Its BlackBerry devices, famous for their physical keyboards and secure email, became hugely popular with business and government users in the 2000s.\n\nAs touchscreen smartphones took over, the company stopped designing its own phones and renamed itself BlackBerry Limited in 2013. It shifted to software, including cybersecurity and QNX, used in vehicles and embedded systems.", lang: "en", topic: "companies" },
  { q: ["What is Bombardier known for?","who founded bombardier","did bombardier invent the snowmobile"], a: "Bombardier was founded by Joseph-Armand Bombardier in Valcourt, Quebec. He developed snow vehicles in the 1930s and launched the personal Ski-Doo snowmobile in 1959.\n\nThe company later expanded into trains and aircraft. Over time it sold or spun off divisions: recreational products became BRP in 2003, and the rail business went to Alstom in 2021. Today Bombardier focuses on business jets. For current news or financials, I can check live.", lang: "en", topic: "companies" },
  { q: ["What are Canada's two big railways?","cn vs cp rail","what is cpkc"], a: "Canada has two major freight railways:\n- CN (Canadian National): created by the federal government around 1918 to 1919 from several struggling railways, privatized in 1995, and headquartered in Montreal.\n- CPKC (Canadian Pacific Kansas City): Canadian Pacific built Canada's first transcontinental railway, with the last spike driven in 1885 at Craigellachie, BC. It combined with Kansas City Southern in 2023, and is based in Calgary.\n\nBoth mainly move freight, not passengers. VIA Rail handles national passenger service.", lang: "en", topic: "companies" },
  { q: ["Tell me about Air Canada","air canada history","when did air canada start"], a: "Air Canada began in 1937 as Trans-Canada Air Lines, a federal Crown corporation, and took the name Air Canada in 1965. It was privatized in 1988 to 1989 and is headquartered in Montreal.\n\nIt's Canada's largest airline and flag carrier, flying domestic, US and international routes, and it's a founding member of the Star Alliance network. For current routes, fares, leadership or news, I can look them up live.", lang: "en", topic: "companies" },
  { q: ["Where is WestJet from?","tell me about westjet","when was westjet founded"], a: "WestJet was founded in 1996 in Calgary, Alberta, as a low-cost airline serving Western Canada. Its early model was inspired by US low-cost carriers.\n\nIt has since grown to fly across Canada and to the US, Mexico, the Caribbean and other international destinations, and it's still headquartered in Calgary. For current routes, fares, ownership or news, I can check live.", lang: "en", topic: "companies" },
  { q: ["Who are the big telecom companies in Canada?","rogers bell telus explained","what are the big three carriers"], a: "Canada's three largest telecom companies are often called the \"Big Three\":\n- Rogers: Toronto-based, started by Ted Rogers, with roots in radio and cable; also owns Fido and other brands.\n- Bell: Bell Canada traces back to 1880; its parent company is BCE, based in Montreal. Brands include Virgin Plus and Lucky Mobile.\n- Telus: Western Canadian roots, formed from Alberta and BC telephone companies; also owns Koodo.\n\nFor current plans or prices, I can look them up live.", lang: "en", topic: "companies" },
  { q: ["What does Loblaw own?","tell me about loblaw","is shoppers drug mart owned by loblaw"], a: "Loblaw Companies was founded in Toronto in 1919 by Theodore Loblaw and J. Milton Cork, with self-serve grocery stores that were new at the time.\n\nToday it's one of Canada's largest food and pharmacy retailers. Its banners include Loblaws, No Frills, Real Canadian Superstore, Provigo and Maxi. It acquired Shoppers Drug Mart in 2014, and its house brands include President's Choice and No Name. For current news or prices, I can check live.", lang: "en", topic: "companies" },
  { q: ["What is Couche-Tard?","who owns circle k","is circle k canadian"], a: "Alimentation Couche-Tard is a Quebec company founded in 1980 by Alain Bouchard, who opened his first store in Laval. It's headquartered in Laval.\n\nThrough acquisitions, it became one of the world's largest convenience store operators. It owns the Circle K brand, used for most of its stores internationally, while many Quebec stores still carry the Couche-Tard name. For current details or news, I can look them up live.", lang: "en", topic: "companies" },
  { q: ["What does Magna International do?","tell me about magna","who founded magna"], a: "Magna International is one of the world's largest automotive suppliers, headquartered in Aurora, Ontario. It was founded by Frank Stronach, who started a tool-and-die shop in Toronto in 1957.\n\nMagna designs and manufactures auto parts and systems, including body and chassis parts, seating, powertrain components, electronics, and even complete vehicles for other automakers on contract. For current leadership or financials, I can check live.", lang: "en", topic: "companies" },
  { q: ["Who founded Apple?","when was apple founded","apple company history"], a: "Apple was founded on April 1, 1976, by Steve Jobs, Steve Wozniak and Ronald Wayne in California. Its first product was the Apple I computer, which Wozniak designed.\n\nApple is headquartered in Cupertino, California, and designs consumer electronics, software and services, including the Mac, iPhone, iPad and Apple Watch. For current products, leadership or financial details, I can look them up live.", lang: "en", topic: "companies" },
  { q: ["Who founded Google?","what is alphabet","google history"], a: "Google was founded in 1998 by Larry Page and Sergey Brin, who met as PhD students at Stanford University. It started as a search engine based on their PageRank method for ranking web pages.\n\nIn 2015, the company reorganized, creating Alphabet as a parent company, with Google as its largest subsidiary. Google's services include Search, Android, YouTube, Maps, Gmail and cloud computing. It's headquartered in Mountain View, California. For current details, I can check live.", lang: "en", topic: "companies" },
  { q: ["When was Microsoft founded?","who started microsoft","microsoft history"], a: "Microsoft was founded in 1975 by Bill Gates and Paul Allen in Albuquerque, New Mexico. It later moved to the Seattle area and is headquartered in Redmond, Washington.\n\nMicrosoft is known for the Windows operating system and Office productivity software, now part of Microsoft 365, as well as Azure cloud computing, Xbox, LinkedIn and GitHub. For current leadership, products or financials, I can look them up live.", lang: "en", topic: "companies" },
  { q: ["How did Amazon start?","who founded amazon","was amazon a bookstore"], a: "Yes, Amazon started as an online bookstore. Jeff Bezos founded it in 1994 in the Seattle area, and it began selling books online in 1995.\n\nIt expanded into nearly every retail category and became a major marketplace for third-party sellers. Other major businesses include Amazon Web Services (cloud computing), Prime, streaming and devices like Kindle and Echo. For current details or news, I can check live.", lang: "en", topic: "companies" },
  { q: ["What is Samsung's history?","where is samsung from","who founded samsung"], a: "Samsung was founded in 1938 by Lee Byung-chul in Daegu, Korea, as a small trading company selling goods like dried fish and noodles. It later expanded into textiles, insurance and many other industries.\n\nSamsung Electronics, founded in 1969, became one of the world's largest makers of smartphones, TVs, memory chips and appliances. The Samsung group is headquartered in South Korea. For current products or financials, I can look them up live.", lang: "en", topic: "companies" },
  { q: ["How did Toyota start?","toyota history","does toyota make cars in canada"], a: "Toyota grew out of Toyoda Automatic Loom Works, a loom maker founded by Sakichi Toyoda. His son, Kiichiro Toyoda, started an automobile division, and Toyota Motor Corporation was established in 1937. It's headquartered in Toyota City, Japan.\n\nToyota is known for the Toyota Production System and lean manufacturing, and for vehicles like the Corolla and Camry. Yes, Toyota builds vehicles in Canada, at plants in Cambridge and Woodstock, Ontario. For current details, I can check live.", lang: "en", topic: "companies" },
  { q: ["Who is the CEO of RBC right now?","what's shopify's stock price","how many employees does air canada have"], a: "Details like CEOs, stock prices, revenue and employee counts change often, so I don't rely on memory for them. I can look up the current information live for you.\n\nWhat I can share reliably is background, like when and where a company was founded and what it does. Just tell me which company you're curious about.", lang: "en", topic: "companies" },
  { q: ["C'est combien un pourboire de 15 % sur 60 $?","pourboire 15% sur 60$","combien je laisse de tip sur une facture de 60 piastres"], a: "Un pourboire de 15 % sur 60 $ donne 9 $.\n\nCalcul : 60 × 0,15 = 9\n\nTon total serait donc 60 $ + 9 $ = 69 $ (avant taxes si le pourboire est calculé sur le montant avant taxes).\n\nTruc rapide : 10 % de 60 $, c'est 6 $; la moitié de ça (5 %), c'est 3 $; 6 $ + 3 $ = 9 $.", lang: "fr", topic: "math" },
  { q: ["Comment résoudre 4x − 6 = 10?","resous 4x-6=10","trouve x dans 4x moins 6 égale 10"], a: "x = 4.\n\nÉtapes :\n1. Ajoute 6 des deux côtés : 4x = 16\n2. Divise les deux côtés par 4 : x = 4\n\nVérification : 4(4) − 6 = 16 − 6 = 10. ✓\n\nL'idée, c'est de défaire les opérations dans l'ordre inverse, en faisant toujours la même chose des deux côtés de l'égalité.", lang: "fr", topic: "math" },
  { q: ["Comment additionner 1/2 et 1/3?","1/2 + 1/3 ça donne quoi","additionner des fractions avec des dénominateurs différents"], a: "1/2 + 1/3 = 5/6.\n\nÉtapes :\n1. Trouve un dénominateur commun : le plus petit multiple commun de 2 et 3, c'est 6.\n2. Convertis : 1/2 = 3/6 et 1/3 = 2/6.\n3. Additionne les numérateurs : 3/6 + 2/6 = 5/6.\n\n5/6 ne se simplifie pas, puisque 5 et 6 n'ont aucun facteur commun.", lang: "fr", topic: "math" },
  { q: ["Quelle est l'aire d'un cercle de 3 cm de rayon?","aire cercle rayon 3","comment calculer l'aire d'un cercle"], a: "L'aire est de 9π cm², soit environ 28,27 cm².\n\nFormule : A = πr²\n\nCalcul : π × 3² = π × 9 ≈ 3,14159 × 9 ≈ 28,27 cm²\n\nÀ noter : la circonférence, c'est C = 2πr = 6π ≈ 18,85 cm. Si on te donne le diamètre, divise-le par 2 pour obtenir le rayon.", lang: "fr", topic: "math" },
  { q: ["C'est quoi le théorème de Pythagore?","pythagore avec des côtés de 5 et 12","comment trouver l'hypoténuse"], a: "Dans un triangle rectangle, a² + b² = c², où c est l'hypoténuse (le côté opposé à l'angle droit).\n\nExemple avec des côtés de 5 et 12 :\n1. 5² + 12² = 25 + 144 = 169\n2. c = √169 = 13\n\nAutres triplets entiers connus : 3-4-5 et 8-15-17. Ça fonctionne seulement pour les triangles qui ont un angle de 90°.", lang: "fr", topic: "math" },
  { q: ["Comment calculer la moyenne et la médiane de 10, 12, 14 et 20?","moyenne mediane exemple","c'est quoi la différence entre moyenne et médiane"], a: "Moyenne = 14; médiane = 13.\n\n- Moyenne : additionne les valeurs et divise par leur nombre. 10 + 12 + 14 + 20 = 56; 56 ÷ 4 = 14\n- Médiane : la valeur du milieu une fois les données en ordre. Avec un nombre pair de valeurs, on fait la moyenne des deux du milieu : (12 + 14) ÷ 2 = 13\n\nLa médiane est moins influencée par les valeurs extrêmes, comme le 20 ici.", lang: "fr", topic: "math" },
  { q: ["Comment convertir des degrés Celsius en Fahrenheit?","25 degrés celsius en fahrenheit","formule celsius fahrenheit"], a: "Utilise F = C × 9/5 + 32.\n\nExemple : 25 °C → 25 × 9/5 + 32 = 45 + 32 = 77 °F\n\nPoints de repère :\n- 0 °C = 32 °F (l'eau gèle)\n- 100 °C = 212 °F (l'eau bout au niveau de la mer)\n- −40 °C = −40 °F (les deux échelles se rejoignent)\n\nPour l'inverse : C = (F − 32) × 5/9.", lang: "fr", topic: "math" },
  { q: ["Peux-tu m'expliquer les intérêts composés avec un exemple?","interets composes formule","2000 $ à 4 % pendant 2 ans ça donne combien"], a: "Avec les intérêts composés, tu gagnes aussi de l'intérêt sur les intérêts déjà accumulés.\n\nFormule : A = P(1 + r/n)^(nt)\n- P = capital, r = taux annuel (en décimal), n = périodes par année, t = années\n\nExemple : 2 000 $ à 4 % composé annuellement pendant 2 ans\nA = 2 000 × 1,04² = 2 000 × 1,0816 = 2 163,20 $\n\nAnnée 1 : 2 080 $; année 2 : 2 163,20 $. C'est un exemple mathématique; pour tes placements, consulte un conseiller financier autorisé.", lang: "fr", topic: "math" },
  { q: ["Pourquoi est-ce qu'on a des saisons?","c'est quoi qui cause l'hiver et l'été","est-ce que c'est parce que la terre est plus proche du soleil en été"], a: "Les saisons sont causées par l'inclinaison de l'axe de la Terre, environ 23,5°, et non par sa distance du Soleil. D'ailleurs, la Terre est la plus proche du Soleil au début de janvier.\n\nQuand l'hémisphère Nord penche vers le Soleil, les rayons arrivent plus directement et les journées sont plus longues : c'est l'été. Quand il penche dans l'autre sens, c'est l'hiver. C'est pour ça que les saisons sont inversées dans l'hémisphère Sud.", lang: "fr", topic: "science" },
  { q: ["Qu'est-ce qui cause les aurores boréales?","aurores boréales explication","pourquoi on voit des aurores au canada"], a: "Les aurores boréales se produisent quand des particules chargées venant du Soleil (le vent solaire) sont dirigées par le champ magnétique terrestre vers les régions polaires. Elles frappent les gaz de la haute atmosphère, qui se mettent à briller.\n\n- L'oxygène donne surtout du vert, parfois du rouge.\n- L'azote peut ajouter du bleu ou du violet.\n\nUne bonne partie du Canada se trouve sous la zone aurorale. Les nuits claires, loin des lumières de la ville, sont idéales.", lang: "fr", topic: "science" },
  { q: ["C'est quoi l'ADN?","explique moi l'adn simplement","comment fonctionne l'adn"], a: "L'ADN (acide désoxyribonucléique) est la molécule qui contient les instructions pour construire et faire fonctionner un être vivant.\n\nIl a la forme d'une échelle torsadée, la double hélice. Les barreaux sont des paires de quatre bases : A, T, C et G (A avec T, C avec G). L'ordre de ces bases forme les gènes, qui indiquent aux cellules comment fabriquer des protéines.\n\nTu hérites de la moitié de ton ADN de chacun de tes parents biologiques.", lang: "fr", topic: "science" },
  { q: ["C'est quoi le pH?","acide ou basique ça veut dire quoi","échelle de ph expliquée"], a: "Le pH mesure à quel point une solution aqueuse est acide ou basique, sur une échelle allant généralement de 0 à 14.\n\n- Moins de 7 : acide (jus de citron, vinaigre)\n- 7 : neutre (eau pure)\n- Plus de 7 : basique (bicarbonate de soude dans l'eau, savon, eau de Javel)\n\nChaque unité représente un facteur de 10 : un pH de 3 est dix fois plus acide qu'un pH de 4. Ne mélange jamais l'eau de Javel avec des acides ou de l'ammoniaque.", lang: "fr", topic: "science" },
  { q: ["C'est quoi la deuxième loi de Newton?","f = ma ça veut dire quoi","force masse accélération exemple"], a: "La deuxième loi de Newton dit que la force égale la masse multipliée par l'accélération : F = ma. Plus tu pousses fort, plus l'objet accélère; plus il est lourd, moins il accélère avec la même force.\n\nExemple : pour accélérer un chariot de 10 kg à 2 m/s², il faut une force de 10 × 2 = 20 newtons.\n\nC'est pour ça qu'un panier d'épicerie plein est plus difficile à faire démarrer qu'un panier vide.", lang: "fr", topic: "science" },
  { q: ["Comment fonctionne la photosynthèse?","c'est quoi la photosynthèse","comment les plantes font leur nourriture"], a: "La photosynthèse, c'est la façon dont les plantes, les algues et certaines bactéries fabriquent leur nourriture grâce à la lumière.\n\nEn résumé : dioxyde de carbone + eau + énergie lumineuse → glucose (sucre) + oxygène\n\nÇa se passe surtout dans les feuilles, dans les chloroplastes, qui contiennent la chlorophylle, le pigment vert qui capte la lumière. La plante utilise le glucose pour son énergie et sa croissance, et rejette de l'oxygène.", lang: "fr", topic: "science" },
  { q: ["Pourquoi on met du sel sur les routes l'hiver?","comment le sel fait fondre la glace","sel de déglaçage explication"], a: "Le sel abaisse le point de congélation de l'eau. Quand il se dissout dans la mince couche d'eau sur la glace, la saumure obtenue doit atteindre une température plus froide pour geler, donc la glace fond.\n\nPar grand froid, c'est moins efficace : le sel de route ordinaire perd beaucoup de son effet sous environ −10 °C. C'est pourquoi on utilise aussi du sable ou d'autres abrasifs. Le sel accélère la rouille, alors laver ta voiture l'hiver aide.", lang: "fr", topic: "science" },
  { q: ["Quelle est la différence entre la météo et le climat?","climat vs météo","s'il fait froid aujourd'hui ça veut dire que le réchauffement climatique existe pas?"], a: "La météo, c'est ce qui se passe dans l'atmosphère maintenant ou dans les prochains jours : la tempête d'aujourd'hui, la température de demain.\n\nLe climat, c'est la tendance à long terme de la météo dans une région, généralement calculée sur 30 ans ou plus.\n\nUne journée froide ne dit rien sur le climat; ce sont les tendances sur plusieurs décennies qui comptent.", lang: "fr", topic: "science" },
  { q: ["C'est quoi le cégep?","comment ça marche le cegep","faut-il aller au cégep pour aller à l'université au québec"], a: "Le cégep (collège d'enseignement général et professionnel) est un niveau d'études propre au Québec, après le secondaire.\n\nIl y a deux grands parcours :\n- Préuniversitaire : généralement 2 ans, habituellement requis pour entrer dans une université québécoise\n- Technique : généralement 3 ans, mène directement au marché du travail, mais peut aussi mener à l'université\n\nDans les cégeps publics, il n'y a pas de droits de scolarité pour les résidents du Québec, mais d'autres frais s'appliquent.", lang: "fr", topic: "education" },
  { q: ["Qui gère l'éducation au Canada?","est-ce que l'école c'est fédéral ou provincial","y a-t-il un ministère de l'éducation canadien"], a: "Au Canada, l'éducation relève des provinces et des territoires, pas du gouvernement fédéral. Chacun a son propre ministère de l'Éducation qui fixe le programme, les exigences pour le diplôme et le calendrier scolaire.\n\nC'est pour ça que la structure des niveaux et le nom des diplômes varient d'une province à l'autre. Au Québec, par exemple, le secondaire se termine en 5e secondaire, puis on va au cégep.", lang: "fr", topic: "education" },
  { q: ["C'est quoi la différence entre un collège et une université?","college ou universite au canada","je devrais aller au collège ou à l'université?"], a: "En général au Canada :\n- Collège : programmes pratiques axés sur un métier (certificats, diplômes, apprentissages), souvent plus courts, avec des stages.\n- Université : diplômes universitaires (baccalauréat, maîtrise, doctorat), avec plus de théorie et de recherche.\n\nAu Québec, le cégep joue un rôle particulier entre le secondaire et l'université. Les deux voies peuvent mener à de belles carrières, et des passerelles existent souvent entre elles.", lang: "fr", topic: "education" },
  { q: ["Comment fonctionne l'aide financière aux études?","prêts et bourses québec","est-ce que je peux avoir des prêts et bourses pour le cégep"], a: "Chaque province a son propre programme d'aide aux étudiants. Au Québec, c'est l'Aide financière aux études (AFE), qu'on appelle souvent « prêts et bourses ». Ailleurs au Canada, les programmes provinciaux sont généralement combinés avec les bourses et prêts fédéraux.\n\nL'aide est surtout basée sur les besoins financiers : tes revenus, ceux de tes parents ou de ton conjoint, et tes dépenses. Les montants et les règles changent, alors vérifie le site du gouvernement de ta province.", lang: "fr", topic: "education" },
  { q: ["C'est quoi la francisation au Québec?","cours de français gratuits pour immigrants","comment apprendre le français au québec"], a: "La francisation, ce sont les cours de français offerts par le Québec aux personnes immigrantes et à d'autres personnes admissibles pour les aider à s'intégrer, travailler et étudier en français.\n\nLes cours existent en plusieurs formules : temps plein, temps partiel, en ligne et en milieu de travail. Une aide financière peut être offerte dans certains cas. Le programme est géré par la province, maintenant en grande partie par Francisation Québec. Vérifie les conditions actuelles sur le site du gouvernement du Québec.", lang: "fr", topic: "education" },
  { q: ["Comment mieux étudier pour mes examens?","trucs pour étudier","j'arrive pas à retenir ce que j'étudie"], a: "Des méthodes qui fonctionnent :\n- Rappel actif : teste-toi au lieu de relire. Utilise des cartes mémoire ou des exercices.\n- Répétition espacée : révise sur plusieurs jours plutôt que la veille.\n- Explique la matière à voix haute, comme si tu l'enseignais.\n- Travaille par blocs de 25 à 50 minutes, avec de courtes pauses.\n- Range ton cellulaire dans une autre pièce.\n- Dors bien : ta mémoire se consolide pendant le sommeil.", lang: "fr", topic: "education" },
  { q: ["Comment écrire une bonne dissertation?","structure d'un texte argumentatif","trucs pour rédiger un essai"], a: "Une structure claire aide beaucoup :\n1. Introduction : amène le sujet, pose-le, puis annonce ta thèse ou ton plan.\n2. Développement : un argument principal par paragraphe, appuyé par des preuves ou des exemples, avec un lien clair vers ta thèse.\n3. Conclusion : fais une synthèse et ouvre sur une réflexion plus large, sans nouvel argument.\n\nFais un plan avant d'écrire, rédige un brouillon, puis révise ton texte et ton orthographe.", lang: "fr", topic: "education" },
  { q: ["Pourquoi je dois citer mes sources?","comment citer ses sources","c'est quoi le plagiat"], a: "Citer tes sources permet de reconnaître le travail des autres, de laisser le lecteur vérifier tes informations et d'éviter le plagiat, que les écoles prennent très au sérieux.\n\nCite chaque fois que tu reprends une citation, une paraphrase, une donnée ou une idée précise de quelqu'un d'autre. Il existe plusieurs normes, comme APA ou MLA. Demande à ton enseignant laquelle utiliser. Paraphraser sans citer la source, c'est aussi du plagiat.", lang: "fr", topic: "education" },
  { q: ["À quoi ressemble un CV au Canada?","cv canadien format","est-ce que je mets ma photo sur mon cv"], a: "Au Canada, un CV fait généralement 1 ou 2 pages et contient :\n- Nom, téléphone, courriel, ville, et ton profil LinkedIn si tu veux\n- Un court profil professionnel\n- Ton expérience, de la plus récente à la plus ancienne, avec tes réalisations\n- Ta formation et tes compétences, dont les langues\n\nN'y mets pas de photo, ni ton âge, ta date de naissance, ton état civil, ta religion ou ton NAS. Adapte ton CV à chaque offre d'emploi.", lang: "fr", topic: "jobs" },
  { q: ["Comment écrire une lettre de présentation?","lettre de motivation conseils","est-ce que la lettre de présentation c'est encore utile"], a: "Oui, ça vaut encore la peine, sauf si l'offre dit le contraire. Garde-la à une page :\n1. Introduction : le poste visé et pourquoi tu es un bon candidat.\n2. Corps : un ou deux paragraphes qui relient ton expérience aux besoins de l'employeur, avec des exemples concrets.\n3. Conclusion : remercie et dis que tu aimerais les rencontrer en entrevue.\n\nAdresse-la à une personne précise si possible, et personnalise chaque lettre.", lang: "fr", topic: "jobs" },
  { q: ["Comment me préparer pour une entrevue?","trucs pour entrevue d'embauche","c'est quoi la méthode STAR"], a: "Prépare-toi sur trois plans :\n1. Recherche : l'entreprise, ses valeurs et les tâches du poste.\n2. Exemples : prépare 5 ou 6 situations avec la méthode STAR : Situation, Tâche, Action (ce que toi tu as fait), Résultat.\n3. Logistique : confirme l'heure, l'adresse ou le lien vidéo, et teste ta technologie.\n\nLe jour même : arrive 10 minutes d'avance, habille-toi un peu plus chic que le milieu de travail, et prépare 2 ou 3 questions à poser.", lang: "fr", topic: "jobs" },
  { q: ["Qui fixe les règles du travail au Québec?","normes du travail québec","c'est quoi la cnesst"], a: "La plupart des milieux de travail sont soumis aux lois de leur province. Au Québec, c'est la Loi sur les normes du travail, appliquée par la CNESST (Commission des normes, de l'équité, de la santé et de la sécurité du travail). Elle encadre le salaire minimum, les heures, les vacances, les congés fériés et les avis de fin d'emploi.\n\nCertains secteurs, comme les banques, les compagnies aériennes et les télécommunications, relèvent plutôt du Code canadien du travail.", lang: "fr", topic: "jobs" },
  { q: ["C'est combien le salaire minimum?","salaire minimum au québec","combien on doit être payé minimum"], a: "Le salaire minimum est fixé par chaque province et territoire, et il existe aussi un salaire minimum fédéral pour les milieux de travail sous réglementation fédérale. Les montants sont ajustés régulièrement, souvent chaque année, et certains emplois ont des taux différents, comme les employés au pourboire.\n\nPour ne pas te donner un chiffre périmé, je peux vérifier le taux actuel pour ta province, ou tu peux consulter le site de la CNESST au Québec.", lang: "fr", topic: "jobs" },
  { q: ["C'est quoi un relevé d'emploi?","comment avoir de l'assurance-emploi","j'ai perdu ma job est-ce que j'ai droit au chômage"], a: "Le relevé d'emploi (RE) est un document que ton employeur doit produire quand tu cesses de recevoir un salaire : mise à pied, démission, congé parental ou de maladie, etc. Service Canada s'en sert pour déterminer si tu as droit à l'assurance-emploi (AE) et à combien.\n\nL'AE offre un revenu temporaire, par exemple si tu perds ton emploi sans que ce soit de ta faute. Il faut assez d'heures assurables. Fais ta demande rapidement; les règles et montants changent, alors vérifie sur canada.ca.", lang: "fr", topic: "jobs" },
  { q: ["C'est quoi un T4 et un relevé 1?","feuillet t4 explication","quand est-ce que je reçois mes papiers d'impôt de mon employeur"], a: "Le T4 est le feuillet fédéral qui résume ton revenu d'emploi de l'année et les retenues faites sur ta paie : impôt, cotisations au RRQ ou au RPC, assurance-emploi, etc. Au Québec, tu reçois aussi un relevé 1 (RL-1) pour ta déclaration provinciale à Revenu Québec.\n\nTon employeur doit te les remettre au plus tard à la fin février pour l'année précédente. Tu en as besoin pour faire tes déclarations de revenus.", lang: "fr", topic: "jobs" },
  { q: ["Comment négocier mon salaire?","trucs pour négocier un salaire","est-ce que je devrais accepter la première offre"], a: "Quelques conseils :\n- Renseigne-toi sur l'échelle salariale du poste dans ta région; le Guichet-Emplois publie des données salariales par profession.\n- Remercie pour l'offre et demande un peu de temps pour y réfléchir.\n- Fais une contre-offre précise, appuyée par tes compétences et ta recherche.\n- Regarde l'ensemble : vacances, horaire flexible, formation, avantages sociaux.\n- Reste positif et collaboratif.\n\nLa plupart des employeurs s'attendent à une certaine négociation.", lang: "fr", topic: "jobs" },
  { q: ["Est-ce que les pneus d'hiver sont obligatoires au Québec?","pneus d'hiver obligatoires quelle date","c'est quand qu'il faut mettre ses pneus d'hiver"], a: "Oui. Au Québec, les pneus d'hiver sont obligatoires pour les véhicules de promenade immatriculés au Québec pendant la période hivernale, actuellement du 1er décembre au 15 mars.\n\nCherche le pictogramme de la montagne avec un flocon de neige : il identifie les vrais pneus d'hiver. La simple mention « M+S » ne suffit pas. Ailleurs au Canada, ils sont fortement recommandés, et la Colombie-Britannique les exige sur plusieurs routes.", lang: "fr", topic: "transport" },
  { q: ["As-tu des conseils pour conduire l'hiver?","conduite hivernale trucs","première fois que je conduis dans la neige"], a: "Conseils pour la conduite hivernale :\n- Roule avec de bons pneus d'hiver.\n- Ralentis et garde beaucoup plus de distance avec le véhicule devant.\n- Accélère, freine et tourne en douceur.\n- Déneige complètement ton auto : vitres, phares et toit.\n- Méfie-toi de la glace noire, surtout sur les ponts.\n- Garde ton réservoir au moins à moitié plein.\n- Vérifie l'état des routes avant de partir avec le service 511.\n- Garde une trousse d'urgence dans ton véhicule.", lang: "fr", topic: "transport" },
  { q: ["C'est quoi la carte OPUS?","comment payer le métro à montréal","carte de transport montréal"], a: "La carte OPUS est la carte à puce rechargeable utilisée pour le transport en commun à Montréal (STM) et dans d'autres réseaux du Québec, dont celui de la Ville de Québec. Tu y charges des titres de transport, comme des passages ou des abonnements mensuels, et tu la valides en montant à bord.\n\nAilleurs au Canada, on utilise par exemple Presto en Ontario et Compass à Vancouver. Les tarifs changent; vérifie le site de ta société de transport.", lang: "fr", topic: "transport" },
  { q: ["Est-ce que je peux échanger mon permis de conduire étranger au Québec?","permis de conduire immigrant saaq","dois-je repasser mon examen de conduite"], a: "Ça dépend du pays qui a délivré ton permis. Le Québec, par la SAAQ, a des ententes de réciprocité avec certains pays et provinces qui permettent d'échanger le permis sans examens. Sinon, tu devras peut-être passer des examens théorique et pratique.\n\nAprès ton arrivée, tu peux généralement conduire avec ton permis étranger valide pendant une période limitée. Vérifie les règles actuelles auprès de la SAAQ, ou de l'organisme de ta province si tu vis ailleurs.", lang: "fr", topic: "transport" },
  { q: ["Comment fonctionne l'assurance auto au Québec?","assurance automobile obligatoire","saaq ou assureur privé"], a: "L'assurance auto est obligatoire partout au Canada et elle est réglementée par chaque province.\n\nAu Québec, le régime est mixte :\n- La SAAQ couvre les blessures corporelles, peu importe qui est responsable de l'accident.\n- Les assureurs privés couvrent les dommages matériels, et l'assurance responsabilité civile est obligatoire.\n\nEn Colombie-Britannique, en Saskatchewan et au Manitoba, l'assurance de base est publique. Les tarifs varient beaucoup; demande des soumissions ou consulte un courtier.", lang: "fr", topic: "transport" },
  { q: ["Est-ce qu'on peut tourner à droite au feu rouge à Montréal?","virage à droite au feu rouge québec","right on red montreal c'est permis?"], a: "Non, pas sur l'île de Montréal : le virage à droite au feu rouge y est interdit.\n\nAilleurs au Québec, il est généralement permis après un arrêt complet, en cédant le passage aux piétons et aux véhicules, sauf si un panneau l'interdit. C'est la même règle de base dans la plupart des autres provinces. Surveille toujours la signalisation à chaque intersection.", lang: "fr", topic: "transport" },
  { q: ["Comment obtenir ma carte d'assurance maladie au Québec?","carte soleil ramq","nouvel arrivant assurance maladie québec"], a: "Ta carte d'assurance maladie (souvent appelée carte soleil) est délivrée par la RAMQ (Régie de l'assurance maladie du Québec). Pour t'inscrire, tu dois généralement fournir une preuve d'identité, une preuve de résidence au Québec et une preuve de ton statut d'immigration ou de citoyenneté.\n\nIl peut y avoir un délai de carence pour certains nouveaux arrivants. Les règles changent, alors vérifie sur le site de la RAMQ. Dans les autres provinces, chaque régime a sa propre carte.", lang: "fr", topic: "health" },
  { q: ["Comment trouver un médecin de famille au Québec?","j'ai pas de médecin de famille","c'est quoi le GAMF"], a: "Inscris-toi au Guichet d'accès à un médecin de famille (GAMF), le système provincial qui te jumelle à un médecin ou une équipe de soins quand une place se libère.\n\nEn attendant :\n- Le Guichet d'accès à la première ligne (GAP) peut t'orienter vers un rendez-vous; on y accède souvent par le 811.\n- Les cliniques sans rendez-vous et les pharmaciens peuvent t'aider pour les problèmes mineurs.\n\nEn cas d'urgence, appelle le 911.", lang: "fr", topic: "health" },
  { q: ["C'est quoi Info-Santé 811?","numéro pour parler à une infirmière","811 québec option 1 et 2"], a: "Au Québec, le 811 te donne accès gratuitement, 24 heures sur 24, à :\n- Info-Santé (option 1) : une infirmière évalue tes symptômes et te dit où consulter.\n- Info-Social (option 2) : un intervenant psychosocial t'aide si tu vis une difficulté.\n\nLa plupart des autres provinces ont aussi une ligne santé au 811. En cas d'urgence, appelle toujours le 911 en premier.", lang: "fr", topic: "health" },
  { q: ["Est-ce qu'un pharmacien peut me prescrire des médicaments?","pharmacien prescrire infection urinaire","je peux voir mon pharmacien au lieu du médecin?"], a: "Oui, dans plusieurs provinces, dont le Québec. Les pharmaciens peuvent évaluer et traiter certaines conditions mineures, comme une infection urinaire simple, une conjonctivite allergique ou des feux sauvages, et souvent prolonger une ordonnance existante.\n\nLa liste des conditions et les frais possibles varient selon la province et changent avec le temps. Appelle ta pharmacie pour savoir ce qu'elle peut faire. Ton pharmacien te dira aussi quand il faut voir un médecin.", lang: "fr", topic: "health" },
  { q: ["Je suis en détresse, j'ai besoin de parler à quelqu'un","ligne de prévention du suicide","j'ai des idées noires"], a: "Merci de m'en parler. Tu peux appeler ou texter le 9-8-8, en tout temps, 24 heures sur 24, pour parler gratuitement et en toute confidentialité à un intervenant formé, en français ou en anglais.\n\nSi tu es en danger immédiat, appelle le 911 ou va à l'urgence la plus proche maintenant.\n\nSi tu veux, tu peux me dire ce qui se passe. Tu n'es pas seul à vivre ça.", lang: "fr", topic: "health" },
  { q: ["Quand est-ce que je dois aller à l'urgence?","urgence ou clinique sans rendez-vous","c'est assez grave pour l'urgence?"], a: "Appelle le 911 ou va à l'urgence pour un problème grave, par exemple :\n- Douleur ou serrement à la poitrine\n- Difficulté à respirer\n- Signes d'AVC : visage affaissé, faiblesse d'un bras, trouble de la parole\n- Saignement important, blessure ou brûlure grave\n- Confusion soudaine ou perte de conscience\n- Réaction allergique sévère\n\nPour un problème moins urgent, essaie une clinique sans rendez-vous, ton pharmacien ou le 811.", lang: "fr", topic: "health" },
  { q: ["Où trouver de l'aide en santé mentale?","consulter un psychologue c'est couvert?","je suis anxieux tout le temps qui peut m'aider"], a: "Plusieurs options :\n- Ton médecin ou une clinique sans rendez-vous peut t'évaluer et te diriger vers des services.\n- Au Québec, le 811 option 2 (Info-Social) offre de l'écoute et de l'orientation.\n- Les CLSC et les organismes communautaires offrent des services.\n- Le programme d'aide aux employés (PAE) de ton employeur inclut souvent des consultations gratuites.\n\nLes psychologues au privé ne sont généralement pas couverts par le régime public. En crise, appelle ou texte le 988.", lang: "fr", topic: "health" },
  { q: ["Où est-ce que je peux me faire vacciner?","vaccin grippe où","comment avoir mes vaccins adulte"], a: "Tu peux généralement te faire vacciner :\n- En pharmacie, qui offre plusieurs vaccins, dont celui contre la grippe saisonnière\n- Chez ton médecin ou une infirmière praticienne\n- Au CLSC ou dans les cliniques de vaccination de la santé publique\n\nAu Québec, la prise de rendez-vous se fait souvent en ligne par Clic Santé. Les vaccins de routine des enfants sont gratuits. Pour savoir lesquels tu devrais recevoir, demande à ton pharmacien ou à la santé publique.", lang: "fr", topic: "health" },
  { q: ["C'est quoi l'intelligence artificielle?","explique moi l'ia","l'ia c'est quoi exactement"], a: "L'intelligence artificielle (IA), c'est une technologie qui permet aux ordinateurs d'accomplir des tâches qui demandent normalement l'intelligence humaine : comprendre le langage, reconnaître des images, faire des prédictions ou résoudre des problèmes.\n\nLa plupart des IA modernes utilisent l'apprentissage automatique : plutôt que de programmer chaque règle, on laisse le système apprendre à partir de grandes quantités d'exemples. L'IA peut se tromper, alors vérifie les informations importantes.", lang: "fr", topic: "technology" },
  { q: ["Comment créer un bon mot de passe?","conseils mots de passe","mot de passe sécuritaire facile à retenir"], a: "Un bon mot de passe est long et unique :\n- Au moins 12 à 15 caractères; la longueur compte le plus.\n- Essaie une phrase de passe faite de 4 mots ou plus choisis au hasard, comme « érable-canot-orbite-tuque » (n'utilise pas celle-là).\n- N'utilise jamais le même mot de passe pour plusieurs comptes.\n- Évite les noms, dates de naissance ou noms d'animaux.\n- Utilise un gestionnaire de mots de passe.\n\nActive aussi l'authentification à deux facteurs.", lang: "fr", topic: "technology" },
  { q: ["C'est quoi l'authentification à deux facteurs?","2fa c'est quoi","devrais-je activer la validation en deux étapes"], a: "L'authentification à deux facteurs (A2F ou 2FA) ajoute une deuxième vérification à ton mot de passe quand tu te connectes. Même si quelqu'un vole ton mot de passe, il ne peut pas entrer sans ce deuxième facteur.\n\nDu plus sûr au moins sûr :\n- Clés de sécurité ou clés d'accès (passkeys)\n- Applications d'authentification qui génèrent des codes\n- Codes par texto (mieux que rien)\n\nActive-la surtout pour ton courriel, ta banque et tes réseaux sociaux.", lang: "fr", topic: "technology" },
  { q: ["C'est quoi l'hameçonnage?","comment reconnaître un courriel frauduleux","phishing c'est quoi"], a: "L'hameçonnage (phishing), c'est quand des fraudeurs se font passer pour une organisation de confiance pour te pousser à cliquer sur un lien, donner tes mots de passe ou envoyer de l'argent.\n\nSignes d'alerte :\n- Urgence ou menaces : « Ton compte sera fermé! »\n- Adresse d'expéditeur qui ne correspond pas à la vraie organisation\n- Liens vers des adresses bizarres\n- Demandes de mots de passe, de codes ou de paiement\n\nDans le doute, ne clique pas : va directement sur le site officiel.", lang: "fr", topic: "technology" },
  { q: ["J'ai reçu un appel de l'ARC qui dit que je vais être arrêté. C'est vrai?","arnaque agence du revenu","texto revenu québec je dois de l'argent"], a: "C'est très probablement une arnaque. L'Agence du revenu du Canada (ARC) et Revenu Québec ne vont pas :\n- Menacer de t'arrêter ou de t'expulser\n- Exiger un paiement en cartes-cadeaux, cartes prépayées ou cryptomonnaie\n- Te demander des renseignements personnels par texto\n\nNe réponds pas et ne clique sur aucun lien. Pour vérifier si tu dois vraiment de l'argent, connecte-toi toi-même à ton dossier en ligne ou appelle au numéro officiel. Signale l'arnaque au Centre antifraude du Canada.", lang: "fr", topic: "technology" },
  { q: ["Est-ce que le Wi-Fi public est sécuritaire?","wifi du café c'est dangereux?","vpn sur wifi public"], a: "Le Wi-Fi public est pratique, mais plus risqué : d'autres personnes sur le même réseau pourraient espionner, et de faux points d'accès portant des noms familiers existent.\n\nConseils :\n- Confirme le nom exact du réseau avec le personnel.\n- Évite les opérations bancaires; utilise plutôt tes données cellulaires.\n- Désactive la connexion automatique aux réseaux ouverts.\n- Un RPV (VPN) fiable ajoute du chiffrement.\n- Garde ton appareil à jour.", lang: "fr", topic: "technology" },
  { q: ["Ça veut dire quoi logiciel libre ou open source?","open source c'est quoi","est-ce que l'open source c'est gratuit"], a: "Un logiciel libre ou à code source ouvert (open source) est un logiciel dont le code est public : tout le monde peut le consulter, l'utiliser, le modifier et le partager, selon les règles de sa licence.\n\nExemples : Linux, Firefox, LibreOffice et Python. Les avantages : transparence, collaboration et souvent aucun coût. Mais l'idée centrale, c'est la liberté d'utiliser et de modifier, pas nécessairement la gratuité; certaines entreprises vendent du soutien autour de ces logiciels.", lang: "fr", topic: "technology" },
  { q: ["Comment faire des sauvegardes de mes fichiers?","backup de mes photos","meilleure façon de sauvegarder mon ordi"], a: "Une bonne règle, c'est le 3-2-1 : garde 3 copies de tes données importantes, sur 2 types de supports différents, dont 1 copie hors de chez toi, par exemple dans le nuage.\n\nOptions :\n- Active la sauvegarde infonuagique intégrée de ton téléphone.\n- Utilise un disque externe avec l'outil de sauvegarde de ton ordinateur.\n- Stocke tes photos et documents dans un service infonuagique.\n\nTeste une restauration de temps en temps pour t'assurer que tes sauvegardes fonctionnent.", lang: "fr", topic: "technology" },
  { q: ["C'est quoi Couche-Tard?","qui possède circle k","couche-tard c'est québécois?"], a: "Oui! Alimentation Couche-Tard est une entreprise québécoise fondée en 1980 par Alain Bouchard, qui a ouvert son premier dépanneur à Laval. Son siège social est toujours à Laval.\n\nGrâce à de nombreuses acquisitions, elle est devenue l'un des plus grands exploitants de dépanneurs au monde. Elle possède la marque Circle K, utilisée pour la plupart de ses magasins à l'international, alors que plusieurs magasins au Québec gardent le nom Couche-Tard. Pour l'actualité, je peux vérifier en direct.", lang: "fr", topic: "companies" },
  { q: ["Qui a fondé Bombardier?","est-ce que bombardier a inventé la motoneige","histoire de bombardier"], a: "Bombardier a été fondée par Joseph-Armand Bombardier, à Valcourt, au Québec. Il a mis au point des autoneiges dans les années 1930, puis lancé la motoneige personnelle Ski-Doo en 1959.\n\nL'entreprise s'est ensuite lancée dans le ferroviaire et l'aéronautique. Les produits récréatifs sont devenus BRP en 2003, et la division ferroviaire a été vendue à Alstom en 2021. Aujourd'hui, Bombardier se concentre sur les avions d'affaires. Pour les nouvelles récentes, je peux vérifier en direct.", lang: "fr", topic: "companies" },
  { q: ["C'est quoi l'histoire de Desjardins?","qui a fondé les caisses desjardins","desjardins c'est une banque?"], a: "Desjardins n'est pas une banque au sens habituel : c'est un groupe financier coopératif, donc appartenant à ses membres. La première caisse populaire a été fondée en 1900 à Lévis par Alphonse Desjardins, avec l'appui de sa femme, Dorimène Desjardins.\n\nLe Mouvement Desjardins offre aujourd'hui des services bancaires, d'assurance, de placement et plus encore, surtout au Québec et en Ontario. Son siège social est à Lévis. Pour l'actualité ou les résultats, je peux vérifier en direct.", lang: "fr", topic: "companies" },
  { q: ["Où a été fondé Shopify?","shopify c'est canadien?","parle moi de shopify"], a: "Oui, Shopify est canadienne. Elle a été fondée à Ottawa en 2006 par Tobias Lütke et Scott Lake. Elle est née d'une boutique en ligne de planches à neige, Snowdevil, pour laquelle Lütke avait créé son propre logiciel de commerce électronique.\n\nShopify offre une plateforme qui permet aux entreprises de créer une boutique en ligne, de vendre en personne et de gérer paiements, expéditions et inventaire. Pour la direction ou les chiffres actuels, je peux vérifier en direct.", lang: "fr", topic: "companies" },
  { q: ["Quelles sont les grandes banques canadiennes?","les 5 grandes banques au canada","la banque nationale c'est une des grandes banques?"], a: "Les « cinq grandes » banques canadiennes sont :\n- RBC (Banque Royale du Canada)\n- TD (Banque Toronto-Dominion)\n- Banque Scotia (Banque de Nouvelle-Écosse)\n- BMO (Banque de Montréal), la plus ancienne banque du pays, fondée en 1817 à Montréal\n- CIBC (Banque Canadienne Impériale de Commerce)\n\nLa Banque Nationale, dont le siège est à Montréal, est souvent considérée comme la sixième. Pour les taux ou les résultats actuels, je peux vérifier en direct.", lang: "fr", topic: "companies" },
  { q: ["Qui est le PDG d'Air Canada?","c'est combien l'action de shopify","combien d'employés chez bombardier"], a: "Les PDG, le cours des actions, les revenus et le nombre d'employés changent souvent, alors je ne me fie pas à ma mémoire pour ces détails. Je peux chercher l'information à jour pour toi en direct.\n\nCe que je peux te donner de façon fiable, c'est le contexte : quand et où l'entreprise a été fondée et ce qu'elle fait. Dis-moi quelle entreprise t'intéresse!", lang: "fr", topic: "companies" },
];
